      "explanation": "The Bezold-Jarisch reflex is a cardio-inhibitory reflex, meaning it slows down and unloads the heart. It is a protective reflex originating from chemoreceptors and mechanoreceptors in the ventricular walls, often triggered by noxious stimuli, certain drugs, or situations like an \"empty\" ventricle in severe hypovolemia. The reflex arc involves vagal afferents and efferents. The classic triad of responses is:\n1. Hypotension (4), caused by peripheral vasodilation (inhibition of sympathetic tone).\n2. Bradycardia (3), caused by a surge in parasympathetic (vagal) output to the heart.\n3. Apnea or Hypopnea (1) (a decrease in respiratory rate or depth).\nTachycardia (2) (a fast heart rate) is the opposite of this reflex. Therefore, the correct features are 1, 3, and 4. This corresponds to option (B).",
