      "paperSession": "May 2025",
      "statement": "Which of the following is an example of feed forward mechanism?",
      "options": [
        "HR increases from supine to standing",
        "Vasoconstriction in response to cooling",
        "Increase in cardiac output in response to anemia",
        "Temperature regulation"
      ],
      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) HR increases from supine to standing",
      "explanation": "A feed-forward mechanism is an anticipatory control system. It initiates a response before a change (or \"error\") in the system is actually detected, in anticipation of that change.\n(A) HR increases from supine to standing: This is the best example. As a person initiates the action of standing, the central nervous system sends signals (central command) that increase heart rate (HR) before the blood pressure has a chance to drop from the effects of gravity. This anticipatory rise helps prevent orthostatic hypotension.\n(B) Vasoconstriction in response to cooling: This is a classic negative feedback loop. The stimulus (cold) is detected, and the body responds (vasoconstriction) to correct the error (heat loss).\n(C) Increase in cardiac output in response to anemia: This is a negative feedback compensation. The body detects the error (low oxygen delivery) and responds (increasing cardiac output) to compensate.\n(D) Temperature regulation: This is a prime example of a system controlled by negative feedback.",
