      "explanation": "The question asks for a technique to determine if two Mycobacterium tuberculosis (M.tb) isolates are from different individuals, which is the fundamental task of molecular epidemiology—tracking an outbreak. This requires a \"fingerprinting\" method to compare the strains.\nRestriction Fragment Length Polymorphism (RFLP) (A) is the classic, gold-standard method for M.tb genotyping. It uses restriction enzymes to cut the bacterial DNA, and the resulting fragments are separated by size. The pattern, often based on the IS6110 insertion sequence, creates a unique \"fingerprint.\" If the RFLP patterns from the two patients are different, it confirms they are infected with different strains and are not part of the same immediate transmission chain.\nMutation analysis (B) and Pyrosequencing (C) are techniques used to look for specific, known mutations (e.g., for drug resistance), not to generate a comprehensive genomic fingerprint. RAPD (D) is another, older fingerprinting method, but RFLP is the traditional standard for M.tb.",
