      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Nitric oxide (NO)",
      "explanation": "Cytochrome C oxidase (Complex IV) is the final enzyme in the electron transport chain, transferring electrons to molecular oxygen. It contains heme iron and copper centers that are targets for several classic poisons. Cyanide (CN) (C) and Hydrogen Sulfide (H₂S) (D) are potent inhibitors that bind to the ferric (Fe³⁺) state of the heme a3 iron, preventing electron transfer and halting aerobic respiration. Carbon Monoxide (CO) (A) also inhibits Complex IV, but it binds to the ferrous (Fe²⁺) state of the heme iron, competing with oxygen for the same binding site. This is the same mechanism by which it binds to hemoglobin.\nIn contrast, Nitric Oxide (NO) (B) is primarily a physiological regulator of Complex IV, not a classic toxic blocker. While very high, non-physiological concentrations of NO can inhibit the enzyme, its normal role is as a reversible, signaling molecule that modulates respiration rates based on cellular needs. Therefore, in the context of classic ETC \"blockers\" or poisons, NO is the exception.",
