      "explanation": "Glucose uptake into cells is mediated by glucose transporters (GLUTs). The most important distinction is between insulin-dependent and insulin-independent transporters. Skeletal muscle (A) and adipose tissue are the primary users of the GLUT4 transporter. GLUT4 is an insulin-dependent transporter, meaning it is stored in intracellular vesicles and only translocates to the cell membrane to facilitate glucose uptake in response to insulin signaling. Therefore, in a state of insulin absence (like Type 1 Diabetes) or insulin resistance (like Type 2 Diabetes), glucose uptake into skeletal muscle is severely impaired.\nIn contrast, the Brain (C) uses GLUT1 (at the blood-brain barrier) and GLUT3 (on neurons), both of which are insulin-independent to ensure a constant, vital supply of glucose. Red blood cells (B) exclusively use insulin-independent GLUT1. The Kidney (D) uses insulin-independent GLUT1 and GLUT2 (as well as SGLT) for glucose reabsorption from the filtrate.",
