      "explanation": "Testing for hereditary cancer syndromes like those caused by BRCA1/2 mutations involves identifying any of a vast number of possible pathogenic variants (mutations) that can occur anywhere along the entire coding sequence of these large genes. The most comprehensive and standard modern method for this is Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS). Whole Exome Sequencing (WES) (B) is a broad NGS technique that sequences all coding regions (exons) of the genome, which would include the BRCA genes. In clinical practice, a more \"targeted\" NGS panel that focuses on BRCA1/2 and other relevant cancer-susceptibility genes is often used.\nRT-PCR (A) and qPCR (C) are methods used to detect and quantify RNA (gene expression), not to identify unknown DNA mutations. Southern blot (D) is an older technique used to detect large-scale DNA rearrangements or deletions. While large deletions can occur in BRCA, they are typically tested for using MLPA (Multiplex Ligation-dependent Probe Amplification), and the primary method for finding the more common point mutations and small insertions/deletions is sequencing.",
