      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Pyroptosis",
      "explanation": "Pyroptosis (A) is a lytic, highly inflammatory form of programmed cell death. It is defined by its dependence on the activation of Caspase-1. Pyroptosis is typically triggered by intracellular pathogens or danger signals, which are sensed by cytosolic inflammasomes (e.g., NLRP3). The inflammasome complex then cleaves pro-Caspase-1 into its active form. Active Caspase-1 has two key functions:\n1. It cleaves the pore-forming protein Gasdermin D, which inserts into the cell membrane and causes cell lysis (oncosis) and the release of intracellular contents.\n2. It cleaves pro-inflammatory cytokines IL-1β and IL-18 into their active, mature forms, which are then released from the lysing cell, driving a potent inflammatory response.\nNecroptosis (B) is also programmed and inflammatory but is caspase-independent (mediated by RIPK1/RIPK3/MLKL). Ferroptosis (C) is iron-dependent cell death via lipid peroxidation. Necrosis (D) is an un-programmed, pathological form of cell death.",
