      "explanation": "Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency is an inherited enzymatic defect that makes red blood cells highly susceptible to oxidative stress. G6PD normally generates NADPH through the pentose phosphate pathway, which helps maintain glutathione in its reduced form. Reduced glutathione protects red blood cells from oxidative damage. When G6PD is deficient, oxidative stress (from infections, certain drugs, or fava beans) leads to hemolysis.\nDuring hemolysis, hemoglobin is released and broken down into heme, which is converted into biliverdin and then into unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin. Because this bilirubin is produced in excess, its level in blood rises. The liver itself is structurally normal and capable of conjugation, so direct (conjugated) bilirubin remains normal. However, increased amounts of unconjugated bilirubin reach the liver and then the intestine after conjugation.\nIn the intestine, bilirubin is converted into urobilinogen. Since more bilirubin is being processed due to hemolysis, more urobilinogen is formed and reabsorbed into the bloodstream, leading to increased urinary urobilinogen levels.\nThus, G6PD deficiency produces indirect hyperbilirubinemia with normal direct bilirubin and raised urine urobilinogen, corresponding to option (D).",
