      "explanation": "Granulosa cell tumor (GCT) is the most common malignant sex cord-stromal tumor of the ovary. It has a set of classic histopathological and immunohistochemical features.\n1. Call-Exner bodies (A) are a pathognomonic finding, representing small, rosette-like structures where granulosa cells arrange around a central eosinophilic, PAS-positive space.\n2. Coffee bean nuclei (D) is the descriptive term for the tumor cell nuclei, which are ovoid and have a prominent longitudinal groove.\n3. Inhibin A (B) is an immunohistochemical stain. As GCTs are derived from granulosa cells, they produce inhibin, and the tumor cells show strong, diffuse positivity for inhibin, making it a key diagnostic marker.\nCA-125 (C) is a tumor marker, both in serum and on immunohistochemistry, that is characteristic of epithelial ovarian cancers (e.g., serous carcinoma). While serum CA-125 can sometimes be elevated in GCT, it is not a specific or characteristic histopathological feature of the tumor cells themselves and is not used for primary diagnosis.",
