      "explanation": "Helicobacter pylori is a spiral-shaped, gram-negative bacterium that colonizes the gastric mucosa, particularly the antrum. Although it is a major cause of peptic ulcer disease and is associated with gastritis, gastric carcinoma, and MALT lymphoma, most infected individuals never develop clinically significant disease.\nThe reason not every infection leads to ulcers is that the majority of infections remain asymptomatic. Many people harbor the organism for years without developing mucosal damage severe enough to produce ulceration. Ulcer formation depends on multiple interacting factors such as bacterial virulence (e.g., CagA, VacA strains), host immune response, gastric acid secretion levels, genetic susceptibility, and environmental influences like smoking or NSAID use.\nIn many individuals, the infection causes only mild chronic gastritis without disrupting the protective mucosal barrier to the extent required for ulcer formation. Therefore, while H. pylori infection is common worldwide, only a small proportion of infected individuals develop peptic ulcers.\nHence, most H. pylori infections do not lead to ulcers because they are asymptomatic and non-ulcerative in the majority of cases.",
