      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) Severe Combined Immunodeficiency (SCID)Transplant rejection is an immune response mediated by the recipient's T-lymphocytes and B-lymphocytes, which recognize the donor graft as foreign. This requires a functional adaptive immune system.",
      "explanation": "Severe Combined Immunodeficiency (SCID) (C) is a group of genetic disorders characterized by a profound failure of both T-cell and B-cell development and function. Patients with \"classic\" SCID have no adaptive immune system and are therefore incapable of mounting an immune response. They cannot recognize or attack a foreign graft, so transplant rejection does not occur. (Instead, they are at high risk of Graft-versus-Host Disease from any T-cells within the transplant).\nBare lymphocyte syndrome (A) is a type of SCID, so it is also correct, but SCID is the broader and more definitive answer. DiGeorge syndrome (D) involves T-cell deficiency but is often partial, so rejection is still possible. Chronic granulomatous disease (B) is a defect in phagocyte (innate) immunity, but T-cell (adaptive) immunity is intact and fully capable of rejection.",
