      "explanation": "A terminal bronchiole is the last part of the conducting portion of the respiratory system. It is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium (often ciliated) and may contain Clara (club) cells. Importantly, its walls do not contain alveoli, which distinguishes it from respiratory bronchioles and alveolar ducts.\nIn the given histology image, the labeled structure appears as a small airway with a relatively continuous wall and a well-defined lumen. There are no outpouchings of alveoli opening directly into its wall. This rules out:\n1. Respiratory bronchiole – would show alveoli budding from its walls.\n2. Alveolar duct – would have almost entirely alveolar walls with very little continuous bronchiolar epithelium.\n3. Bronchus – would contain cartilage plates and submucosal glands, which are absent here.\nSince the structure lacks cartilage, lacks glands, and does not show alveoli arising from its wall, it corresponds to a terminal bronchiole, the final purely conducting airway before the respiratory portion begins.",
