      "answerText": "(C) Cancer cells rely on anaerobic glycolysis even in presence of oxygen (Warburg effect)",
      "explanation": "The phenomenon known as the Warburg effect, described by Otto Warburg, explains the altered metabolism of cancer cells. Normally, in the presence of oxygen, healthy cells generate energy primarily through oxidative phosphorylation in mitochondria, which is efficient and produces large amounts of ATP. However, cancer cells preferentially use glycolysis to generate energy, even when adequate oxygen is available. This process is termed “aerobic glycolysis.”\nAlthough glycolysis produces far less ATP per glucose molecule compared to oxidative phosphorylation, cancer cells compensate by markedly increasing glucose uptake and glycolytic rate. The metabolic intermediates produced during glycolysis are diverted into biosynthetic pathways that support rapid cell proliferation, such as nucleotide, lipid, and amino acid synthesis.\nThus, the key explanation is not increased mitochondrial activity or oxidative phosphorylation, but rather the paradoxical reliance on glycolysis despite oxygen availability. This metabolic reprogramming supports tumor growth and is the basis for FDG-PET imaging in oncology.",
