      "paperSession": "May 2025",
      "statement": "In which disease is an HLA-mismatched transplant not considered problematic?",
      "options": [
        "Bare lymphocyte syndrome",
        "Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome",
        "Job's syndrome",
        "Chediak-Higashi syndrome"
      ],
      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Bare lymphocyte syndrome",
      "explanation": "An HLA-mismatched transplant normally fails due to Graft Rejection (recipient T-cells attack the graft) or Graft-versus-Host Disease (GVHD) (donor T-cells attack the recipient). This question asks in which condition this \"problem\" doesn't occur.\nBare Lymphocyte Syndrome (BLS) (A) is a form of Severe Combined Immunodeficiency (SCID). In BLS Type II (the most common form), there is a genetic defect that prevents the expression of MHC Class II molecules. Without MHC II, the patient's antigen-presenting cells cannot activate CD4+ \"helper\" T-cells. This results in a profound T-cell immunodeficiency, meaning the patient cannot mount an immune response to reject the graft. Furthermore, because the recipient's own tissues also lack MHC II, the donor T-cells in the graft have a reduced ability to recognize the host, significantly mitigating the risk of GVHD. This unique situation makes BLS one of the few conditions where an HLA-mismatched transplant can be successful.\nThe other conditions (B, C, D) are all immunodeficiencies, but T-cell function is sufficiently intact to cause either rejection or severe GVHD.",
