      "paperSession": "May 2025",
      "statement": "What is the role of Pro calcitonin in pneumonia?",
      "options": [
        "Guiding initiation of antibiotic",
        "Early antibiotic discontinuation",
        "Requirement of further investigation",
        "No role"
      ],
      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Early antibiotic discontinuation",
      "explanation": "Procalcitonin (PCT) is a biomarker that is highly specific for systemic bacterial infections. Its levels rise significantly in response to bacterial endotoxins but typically remain low in viral infections or non-infectious inflammatory states. This makes it very useful in managing respiratory infections like pneumonia.\nWhile a very low PCT level can guide initiation (A) (by suggesting a viral cause where antibiotics may be withheld), its most robust, evidence-based, and widely adopted role is in guiding antibiotic discontinuation (B). In a patient being treated for bacterial pneumonia, PCT levels will fall as the infection is controlled. Serial monitoring of PCT allows clinicians to identify when the infection has resolved, even if the patient has other reasons for inflammation (e.g., post-operative state). Following a PCT-guided algorithm (e.g., stopping antibiotics when PCT drops by >80% from its peak) has been shown to safely reduce the total duration of antibiotic therapy, which helps minimize side effects and antibiotic resistance.",
