      "answerText": "(D) Cytochrome C oxidase",
      "explanation": "Cyanide (CN) and Carbon Monoxide (CO) are classic inhibitors of the mitochondrial electron transport chain (ETC). Both substances act by binding to and inhibiting Cytochrome C oxidase (Complex IV) (D), the final enzyme complex in the chain. This enzyme is responsible for transferring electrons from cytochrome c to molecular oxygen (the final electron acceptor) to form water. Cyanide binds to the ferric (Fe³⁺) state of the heme iron in Complex IV, while carbon monoxide binds to the ferrous (Fe²⁺) state. In both cases, this binding prevents electron transfer to oxygen, effectively halting aerobic respiration and leading to rapid cytotoxic hypoxia and cell death. NADH dehydrogenase (A) is Complex I. Succinate dehydrogenase (B) is Complex II. Cytochrome C oxidoreductase (C) is Complex III.",
