      "answerText": "(B) 2, 1, 4, 3",
      "explanation": "Conducting a research study follows a logical, sequential process.\n1. Frame a research question (2): This is the very first step. It identifies the gap in knowledge or the specific problem the research aims to address (e.g., \"Is drug X effective for disease Y?\").\n2. Decide study objective (1): Based on the broad question, you formulate a specific, measurable, and achievable objective (e.g., \"To compare the 6-month mortality rate in patients with disease Y treated with drug X versus placebo.\"). The question comes before the specific objective.\n3. Select study setting (4): You decide on the \"where\" and \"who\" in broad terms. Will this be a hospital-based study, a community-based study, or a multi-center trial? This defines the population from which you will draw your sample.\n4. Select study sample (3): After defining the setting and target population, you apply specific inclusion/exclusion criteria and a sampling strategy to select the final study participants.\nThis logical flow—from broad question, to specific objective, to location, to final sample—is represented by the order 2, 1, 4, 3 (B).",
