      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) 22%",
      "explanation": "Incidence is a measure of the risk of developing a new condition within a specified period. The numerator for incidence is the number of new cases. The denominator is the population at risk at the beginning of the period.\n1. Numerator (New Cases) : 20 new cases of measles.\n2. Population at Risk : The \"population at risk\" includes only those who are susceptible to the disease. The 100 people in the village are the total population. However, 10 of them \"had a history of measles in the past,\" meaning they are immune and not at risk of getting measles again. Therefore, the true population at risk is: 100 (total) - 10 (immune) = 90 people. Incidence Calculation: Incidence = (New Cases / Population at Risk) Incidence = (20 / 90)  100% = 0.2222 × 100% = 22.22% The closest answer is 22% (C).",
