      "answerText": "(B) Paired t-test",
      "explanation": "This question requires selecting the correct statistical test. The key features of the study design are:\n1. Data Type: The outcome is \"mean weight,\" which is continuous (ratio) data. This rules out the Chi-square test (D), which is used for categorical data.\n2. Number of Groups: There is one group of patients.\n3. Nature of Measurement: Measurements are taken on the same individuals at two different time points (\"before\" and \"after\"). This means the data points are paired or dependent.\nThe Paired t-test (B) is the specific statistical test designed to compare the means of two related (paired) samples of continuous data. It assesses whether the mean difference between the \"before\" and \"after\" measurements is significantly different from zero. The Unpaired t-test (A) would be used if you were comparing two different, independent groups (e.g., an intervention group and a control group). ANOVA (C) is used to compare the means of three or more groups.",
