      "answerText": "(A) Mean, median - Dispersion",
      "explanation": "This question tests the classification of statistical measures.\nA. Mean, median - Dispersion: This is incorrect. The Mean and Median are measures of Central Tendency. They describe the \"center\" or \"typical\" value of a dataset. Measures of Dispersion (or variability) include standard deviation, variance, and range, which describe how \"spread out\" the data is.\nB. Standard error - Variation: (Correct). The Standard Error (e.g., Standard Error of the Mean) is a measure of the statistical variation or precision of a sample statistic, estimating how much the sample mean is likely to vary from the true population mean.\nC. Correlation coefficient - Relationship: (Correct). The correlation coefficient (e.g., Pearson's r) is the primary statistic used to quantify the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two continuous variables.\nD. Moments - Skewness: (Correct). In statistics, \"moments\" are quantitative measures. The third central moment is used to calculate skewness (a measure of asymmetry), and the fourth central moment is used to calculate kurtosis.",
