      "explanation": "This is a classic ENT question linking anatomy to clinical symptoms.\n1. Symptom: The \"foreign body sensation\" is a sensory symptom.\n2. Location: The pyriform fossa is located in the supraglottic larynx.\nThe sensory innervation of the larynx is divided:\nAbove the vocal cords (supraglottis), including the pyriform fossa, is supplied by the Internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve (B). This nerve is the afferent (sensory) limb of the cough reflex for this area. Irritation or inflammation (as from a fish bone) in this area is sensed by this nerve, causing the foreign body sensation.\nBelow the vocal cords (subglottis) is supplied by the Recurrent laryngeal nerve (A).\nThe External branch of the superior laryngeal nerve (C) is motor, supplying the cricothyroid muscle.\nThe Glossopharyngeal nerve (D) supplies the oropharynx (tonsils, posterior tongue).\nThe \"change in voice\" (hoarseness) is likely due to inflammatory edema spreading to the nearby vocal cords, but the sensation is from the internal laryngeal nerve.",
