      "explanation": "Juvenile Nasopharyngeal Angiofibroma (JNA) is a benign but highly vascular and locally aggressive tumor of the nasopharynx.\n(B) Epistaxis: Recurrent, severe, and unilateral epistaxis (nosebleed) is the most common presenting symptom, due to the tumor's extreme vascularity.\n(C) Only seen in young boys: This is a key demographic feature. JNA occurs almost exclusively in adolescent and pre-pubescent males.\n(A) Unilateral cervical lymph nodes: While the tumor itself is in the nasopharynx, it can cause nasal obstruction and serous otitis media. Lymph node involvement is not a typical feature, as it's a benign (non-metastasizing) tumor. However, the most incorrect and dangerous option is (D).\n(D) Biopsy: A biopsy of a suspected JNA in an outpatient or office setting is absolutely contraindicated. The tumor is a non-encapsulated mass of blood vessels. Attempting to biopsy it can provoke catastrophic, life-threatening hemorrhage that is extremely difficult to control. The diagnosis is made based on the classic clinical presentation (a young male with recurrent epistaxis and nasal obstruction) and characteristic findings on CT/MRI imaging (e.g., the Holman-Miller sign), often confirmed with angiography.",
