      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) 1 and 2",
      "explanation": "A cochlear implant is a neuroprosthetic device that bypasses the damaged inner ear (cochlea) to directly stimulate the auditory nerve. It is a treatment for severe-to-profound sensorineural hearing loss (SNHL), where the hair cells in the cochlea are non-functional.\n1. Ototoxicity (e.g., from aminoglycoside antibiotics or chemotherapy) causes destruction of cochlear hair cells, resulting in SNHL. This is a prime indication.\n2. Congenital hearing loss (e.g., from genetic causes or infections) is very often sensorineural and is a primary indication for cochlear implantation in children to facilitate speech and language development.\n3. Ossicular fixation (fixation of the middle ear bones) and Otosclerosis (fixation of the stapes bone) are both causes of conductive hearing loss (CHL). In CHL, the inner ear (cochlea) is healthy, but sound cannot be conducted to it. These conditions are treated with surgery (e.g., stapedectomy) or conventional hearing aids, not cochlear implants.",
