      "explanation": "The pulmonary function tests (PFTs) provide the diagnosis.\n1. FEV1/FVC ratio of 65%: A ratio less than 70% (or 0.70) is the defining characteristic of an obstructive lung disease (e.g., COPD, fitting the chronic smoker history). This rules out Restrictive lung disease (C), which would have a normal or high ratio.\n2. RV/TLC ratio of 141%: The RV (Residual Volume) is the air left in the lungs after a full exhalation. TLC is Total Lung Capacity. In obstructive disease, airflow is limited (especially on expiration), leading to air trapping. This means the RV increases significantly. An increased RV/TLC ratio is the quantitative measure of air trapping, which in turn leads to hyperinflation of the lungs (an elevated TLC). While emphysema (a type of COPD) involves increased lung compliance (D), the PFT results directly describe air trapping and hyperinflation.",
