      "explanation": "This 65-year-old man with Parkinson's disease has oropharyngeal dysphagia leading to recurrent aspiration pneumonitis. Chronic aspiration and impaired cough clearance have resulted in bilateral Bronchiectasis. In bronchiectasis, retained secretions promote persistent infection and inflammation, creating a cycle of mucus stasis and airway damage.\nThe key long-term management principle in bronchiectasis is airway clearance therapy. High-frequency chest wall oscillation (HFCWO) improves mucus mobilization and enhances expectoration, thereby reducing infection frequency and further lung damage. In patients with neuromuscular disorders like Parkinson’s disease, impaired cough and secretion clearance make mechanical airway clearance especially beneficial.\nNebulized N-acetylcysteine may thin secretions but does not adequately address impaired clearance. PEG tube placement can reduce future aspiration risk but does not treat established bronchiectasis. Long-term macrolide therapy is considered for frequent infective exacerbations but is not first-line for secretion management.\nTherefore, the most appropriate long-term strategy to manage bronchiectasis in this context is high-frequency chest wall oscillation therapy.",
