      "explanation": "This patient has blood pressure readings of 148/90 mmHg and 152/96 mmHg on two separate occasions. According to current hypertension guidelines (including Indian and WHO recommendations for primary care settings), this falls under Stage 1 hypertension (140–159/90–99 mmHg).\nIn asymptomatic patients without diabetes, chronic kidney disease, cardiovascular disease, or other target organ damage, immediate pharmacologic therapy is not mandatory at this stage. The recommended first step is:\nLifestyle modification, including salt restriction, weight reduction, regular physical activity, smoking cessation, moderation of alcohol intake, and dietary changes (DASH diet).\n1. Drug therapy is generally initiated if:\n2. Persistent BP ≥160/100 mmHg (Stage 2 hypertension), or\n3. Stage 1 hypertension with high cardiovascular risk or comorbidities.\n4. Immediate IV antihypertensives are reserved for hypertensive emergencies, which this patient does not have. Referral is unnecessary in uncomplicated mild hypertension.\nTherefore, the most appropriate next step in this asymptomatic individual is observation and lifestyle advice, initiating medication only if BP exceeds 160/100 mmHg or remains persistently elevated.",
