      "explanation": "In a patient with Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) on mechanical ventilation, the development of sudden respiratory distress, absent or markedly decreased breath sounds on one side (in this case, the right), and tracheal deviation to the opposite side are classic signs of a tension pneumothorax. This occurs due to barotrauma, a well-known complication of positive pressure ventilation.\nHigh airway pressures used to maintain oxygenation in ARDS can cause alveolar overdistension and rupture, allowing air to escape into the pleural cavity. The trapped air collapses the affected lung (right side here) and exerts pressure on the mediastinum, shifting the trachea and heart to the opposite side (left), leading to hemodynamic compromise and hypoxia.\nThis is an emergency requiring immediate decompression, typically with a needle thoracostomy followed by chest tube insertion.\nOther options are incorrect:\n(B) Increased tidal volume may cause barotrauma but is not itself a diagnosis.\n(C) Oxygen toxicity causes diffuse lung injury, not unilateral findings.\n(D) ET malposition would cause decreased breath sounds on the opposite side (usually left) with tracheal shift towards the affected side.",
