      "explanation": "The Rotterdam criteria (2003) are the most widely used diagnostic criteria for Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS). The diagnosis requires the presence of two out of the following three features:\n1. Oligo- and/or anovulation: This manifests clinically as infrequent or absent menstrual periods, which is described as \"Reduced cycles\" (b).\n2. Clinical and/or biochemical signs of hyperandrogenism: This includes clinical signs like \"Hirsutism\" (a) (excessive male-pattern hair growth), acne, or alopecia, or biochemical signs like elevated free or total testosterone.\n3. Polycystic ovaries on ultrasound: Defined as 12 or more follicles in each ovary measuring 2-9 mm in diameter, and/or increased ovarian volume.\nWhile Insulin resistance (c) and Obesity (d) are very common features and part of the metabolic syndrome associated with PCOS, they are not part of the formal Rotterdam diagnostic criteria. Therefore, the correct pair from the criteria is (a) and (b).",
