      "answerText": "(A) Dinoprostone",
      "explanation": "Postpartum Hemorrhage (PPH) is most commonly caused by uterine atony (a \"boggy,\" non-contracted uterus). Management involves the urgent administration of uterotonic drugs to stimulate strong uterine contractions. The first-line agent is Oxytocin (D), given as an infusion. If this fails, second-line agents are used. These include Carboprost (an analogue of Methyl PGF2 alpha) (B) and Misoprostol (C) (a PGE1 analogue), which is particularly useful in low-resource settings as it is oral/rectal and stable. Methylergometrine is also used.\nIn contrast, Dinoprostone (A) is a PGE2 analogue. While it is a prostaglandin, its primary use in obstetrics is for cervical ripening and labor induction. It is not used in the management of PPH. Its formulation (as a vaginal gel or insert) and slower-acting, localized effect on the cervix make it unsuitable for the emergency of PPH, which requires rapid, powerful, systemic contraction of the entire uterine body.",
