      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Maternal fever",
      "explanation": "Fetal bradycardia (a heart rate < 110 bpm) is a non-reassuring sign, typically indicating fetal distress or hypoxia. Abruptio placenta (A) causes profound hypoxia and distress, leading to bradycardia. Fetal head compression (D) (especially early in labor) can cause a vagal nerve response, resulting in a temporary bradycardia known as an \"early deceleration.\" Meconium aspiration (C) is a sign of fetal distress, and the underlying hypoxia that causes meconium passage is also a cause of bradycardia.\nIn contrast, Maternal fever (B) does not cause fetal bradycardia. Maternal fever, most often from chorioamnionitis, causes a systemic inflammatory state and increases the metabolic rate of both the mother and the fetus. The fetus responds to this hyperthermic and inflammatory environment with an increased heart rate, leading to fetal tachycardia (a heart rate > 160 bpm). This is the opposite of bradycardia.",
