      "explanation": "Magnesium sulfate (MgSO₄) is the drug of choice in pre-eclampsia and eclampsia primarily for seizure prophylaxis and treatment. Its main mechanism is central nervous system depression through NMDA receptor blockade and reduction of neuromuscular transmission, thereby decreasing seizure incidence (C). This is its most important clinical role.\nMgSO₄ also provides fetal neuroprotection (A) when administered in women at risk of imminent preterm delivery (usually before 32 weeks). It reduces the risk of cerebral palsy in preterm infants.\nHowever, magnesium sulfate does not prevent placental abruption (B). Abruption occurs due to severe hypertension and vascular pathology; MgSO₄ does not directly prevent this complication.\nAlthough magnesium has mild smooth muscle–relaxant properties and was historically used as a tocolytic, it is not effective for preventing preterm labour and is no longer recommended for this purpose.\nSince prevention of preterm labour is not a therapeutic indication of MgSO₄ in pre-eclampsia, the correct answer is : (D) Prevent preterm labour.",
