      "explanation": "An Intrauterine Contraceptive Device (IUCD), including the levonorgestrel-containing IUS (e.g., Mirena), can be inserted at several time points to ensure the woman is not pregnant. The ideal time is (B) Within 7 days of menstruation (i.e., during or just after her period), as this is when the cervical os is slightly open and pregnancy is least likely. It can also be inserted (D) immediately post-delivery (within 10 minutes of placental delivery) or (A) within 48 hours of delivery (postpartum insertion).\nThe one option that is incorrect and unsafe is (C) Within 10 days of the next menstrual cycle. This phrasing is confusing, but it implies insertion in the middle of the follicular phase (e.g., day 10). Inserting an IUCD at this time, without confirming non-pregnancy, carries a significant risk of insertion into an already (and unknowingly) pregnant uterus. The \"7-day rule\" applies from the start of the cycle, not 10 days into it.",
