      "explanation": "In a Lineweaver–Burk plot (double reciprocal plot of 1/V versus 1/[S]), different types of enzyme inhibition show characteristic patterns.\nFor Inhibitor A, the lines with and without inhibitor intersect at the Y-axis. This indicates that Vmax remains unchanged, while Km increases. Since competitive inhibitors compete with the substrate for the active site, increasing substrate concentration can overcome the inhibition. Therefore, Vmax is unaffected, but Km increases due to reduced apparent affinity. This pattern represents competitive inhibition.\nFor Inhibitor B, the lines intersect on the X-axis. This shows that Km remains unchanged, but Vmax decreases. Non-competitive inhibitors bind to an allosteric site rather than the active site, and their effect cannot be reversed by increasing substrate concentration. Because enzyme activity is reduced regardless of substrate concentration, Vmax decreases while Km remains constant. This pattern represents non-competitive inhibition.\nThus, Inhibitor A is competitive and Inhibitor B is non-competitive, making option (A) correct.",
