      "explanation": "Uncomplicated Plasmodium falciparum malaria is primarily treated with artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT). Drugs such as artemether (usually combined with lumefantrine) are first-line treatments because they rapidly reduce parasite load and are highly effective against resistant strains. Sulphadoxine–pyrimethamine has also been used in combination regimens, although resistance limits its use in many regions.\nChloroquine was previously used for P. falciparum, but widespread resistance has made it ineffective in most endemic areas; however, it may still be used in specific regions where sensitivity is documented.\nPrimaquine, on the other hand, is not used for treating uncomplicated P. falciparum blood-stage infection. Its primary role is in eliminating hypnozoites (dormant liver forms) of Plasmodium vivax and Plasmodium ovale to prevent relapse. In P. falciparum, primaquine may be given as a single low dose to reduce gametocyte carriage and transmission, but it is not used as a standard treatment for uncomplicated infection.\nTherefore, primaquine is the correct exception.",
