      "explanation": "The hierarchy of evidence is a model used to rank the relative strength of results obtained from different types of research studies.\nAt the pinnacle of this hierarchy are Systematic Reviews and Meta-analyses (D). A systematic review critically appraises all relevant studies on a specific topic, and a meta-analysis uses statistical methods to combine the results of several of these studies (ideally, multiple high-quality RCTs). This synthesis provides a more powerful and precise estimate of an intervention's effect than any single study alone.\nA single Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT) (C) is the next highest level and is considered the gold standard for a primary experimental study.\nBelow RCTs are observational studies, with Cohort studies (B) generally providing stronger evidence than Case-control studies (A).\nExpert opinion and case reports are at the bottom of the pyramid.",
