      "explanation": "In laparoscopy, proper port placement is crucial for optimal visualization and instrument handling. The azimuth angle refers to the angle formed between the telescope (camera port) and the working instrument port in the horizontal plane. It represents the degree of separation between the optical axis and the instrument axis around the target organ.\nAn appropriate azimuth angle ensures good triangulation, allowing effective depth perception, precise dissection, and ergonomic movement of instruments. Ideally, this angle is maintained around 30–60 degrees to provide adequate visualization and minimize instrument clashing.\nThe elevation angle refers to the vertical angle between the instrument and the target organ. The manipulation angle is the angle between two working instruments. The term working angle is less specific and not the standard term for the angle between the telescope and the working port.\nThus, the angle formed between the laparoscopic working port and the telescope is called the azimuth angle, making option (A) correct.",
