      "explanation": "The ductus arteriosus is a fetal blood vessel that connects the pulmonary artery to the aorta, allowing blood to bypass the non-functional fetal lungs. In utero, the patency of the ductus is actively maintained by low oxygen tension and high levels of circulating prostaglandins (PGE2). After birth, with the infant's first breaths, the lungs expand, and the arterial oxygen tension (PaO₂) rises dramatically. This high oxygen tension is the single most important and primary stimulus for the functional closure of the ductus arteriosus. The increased oxygen causes the smooth muscle in the wall of the ductus to constrict. Additionally, the drop in pulmonary vascular resistance and the removal of the placenta (the source of PGE2) contribute to the closure process, which typically occurs within the first 24-72 hours of life.",
