      "explanation": "Telogen effluvium is a common form of non-scarring diffuse hair loss that occurs as a reaction to a significant physiological or emotional stressor. The stressor causes a large number of hair follicles in the growing (anagen) phase to prematurely shift into the resting (telogen) phase. Approximately 2 to 4 months after the triggering event, these telogen hairs are shed, resulting in a noticeable increase in hair fall. A significant systemic illness with high fever, such as a COVID-19 infection, is a well-recognized and very common trigger for telogen effluvium. The timing in this case, with hair loss beginning 8 weeks (2 months) after the COVID infection, fits the classic timeline perfectly. Anagen effluvium (C) is an abrupt shedding of growing hairs, typically seen within days of exposure to chemotherapy. Trichotillomania (trichomania) and androgenic alopecia have different presentations.",
