      "explanation": "The given image is a non-contrast CT scan of the head showing a hyperdense (bright) collection adjacent to the inner table of the skull. The hemorrhage appears biconvex (lentiform) in shape and is limited by cranial sutures. This characteristic appearance is typical of an extradural (epidural) hemorrhage.\nExtradural hemorrhage usually occurs due to rupture of the middle meningeal artery, most commonly following temporal bone trauma. Because the blood collects between the skull and the dura mater, it forms a lens-shaped collection that does not cross suture lines, as the dura is tightly adherent at sutures. Clinically, it may present with a history of head injury, brief loss of consciousness, a lucid interval, and subsequent neurological deterioration due to raised intracranial pressure.\nIn contrast, a subdural hemorrhage appears crescent-shaped and can cross sutures. Subarachnoid hemorrhage is seen within sulci and cisterns, while intraventricular hemorrhage is located within the ventricular system.\nTherefore, based on the characteristic biconvex hyper dense lesion seen on CT, the correct diagnosis is (A) Extradural Hemorrhage.",
