      "explanation": "To calculate the Anion Gap (AG), we use the formula: Anion Gap = (Na⁺ + K⁺) − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻) Now substitute the given values: Na⁺= 145 mEq/L K⁺= 4 mEq/L Cl⁻= 90 mEq/L HCO₃⁻= 15 mEq/L Step 1: Add sodium and potassium 145 + 4\t= 149 Step 2: Add chloride and bicarbonate 90 + 15\t= 105 Step 3: Subtract 149 − 105\t= 44 So, the calculated anion gap is 44 mEq/L. However, in many clinical settings potassium is often omitted from the formula: AG\t= Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻) 145\t− (90 + 15) 145\t− 105 = 40 Since most MCQs use the formula without potassium, the correct answer is : (B) 40 This indicates a high anion gap metabolic acidosis, which can be seen in conditions like diabetic ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis, or renal failure.",
