      "explanation": "The image shows a common ophthalmological diagnostic procedure. Fluorescein is a dye that stains areas of the cornea where the epithelial layer is absent, such as in an abrasion or an ulcer. The dye pools in the defect and, when illuminated with a cobalt blue light, it fluoresces, appearing as a bright green or yellow-green area. This makes the epithelial defect clearly visible against the unstained cornea and the dark pupil. The condition depicted is a corneal ulcer or abrasion. Trypan blue is used for staining the lens capsule during cataract surgery. Rose bengal and Lissamine green are vital stains that stain devitalized epithelial cells and mucus, and they are used in the diagnosis of dry eye syndrome and herpetic keratitis. The classic combination for detecting a corneal epithelial defect is fluorescein dye and a cobalt blue light filter.",
