      "answerText": "(A) With the rule astigmatism",
      "explanation": "The prescription +1.00 D sphere with −2.00 D cylinder at 180° represents with-the-rule (WTR) astigmatism.\nAstigmatism occurs when the cornea has unequal curvature in different meridians, causing light rays to focus at different points. In a cylindrical prescription, the axis indicates the meridian that has no cylindrical power, and the cylinder power acts 90° away from that axis.\nHere, the cylinder axis is 180°, meaning the cylindrical correction acts at 90° (vertical meridian). Since the cylinder power is negative (−2.00 D), it corrects excess refractive power in the vertical meridian. This implies that the vertical meridian is steeper than the horizontal meridian, which is the defining feature of with-the-rule astigmatism.\nIn with-the-rule astigmatism, the cornea is more curved vertically and flatter horizontally. It is called “with-the-rule” because this pattern follows the normal corneal curvature seen in younger individuals.\nThe +1.00 D sphere component indicates underlying hyperopia, but the key determinant of astigmatism type is the cylinder axis at 180°.\nTherefore, the correct answer is : (A) With-the-rule astigmatism.",
