      "explanation": "On the ECG strip, there are regular P waves occurring at a constant rate, but not every P wave is followed by a QRS complex. Some atrial impulses fail to conduct to the ventricles, resulting in dropped QRS complexes. The PR intervals of the conducted beats appear constant rather than progressively lengthening. This pattern is characteristic of Mobitz type II second-degree AV block, which usually occurs below the AV node, in the His-Purkinje system (infra-nodal level).\nIn contrast, first-degree heart block shows prolonged PR interval but no dropped beats. Complete heart block demonstrates total AV dissociation, where atrial and ventricular rhythms are independent. AV dissociation can occur in various settings and does not necessarily imply complete block.\nMobitz type II is clinically significant because it can progress to complete heart block and often requires pacemaker insertion. The presence of sudden non-conducted P waves with constant PR intervals in conducted beats supports the diagnosis of second-degree infra-nodal (Mobitz type II) heart block.",
