      "explanation": "A patient who develops watery diarrhea while taking ampicillin most likely has antibiotic-associated diarrhea due to overgrowth of Clostridioides difficile. Broad-spectrum antibiotics like ampicillin disrupt the normal intestinal flora, allowing C. difficile to proliferate and produce toxins (toxin A and toxin B). These toxins cause inflammation of the colon, leading to diarrhea, abdominal pain, and sometimes pseudomembranous colitis.\nCurrent guidelines recommend Fidaxomicin as the preferred first-line treatment for an initial episode of C. difficile infection. Fidaxomicin is a narrow-spectrum, bactericidal antibiotic that specifically targets C. difficile with minimal effect on normal gut flora. This selective action results in lower recurrence rates compared to oral vancomycin. It is administered orally and acts locally within the gastrointestinal tract.\nMetronidazole is no longer first-line except when other agents are unavailable. Ciprofloxacin may worsen the condition. Bezlotoxumab is used only to prevent recurrence, not as primary therapy. Thus, fidaxomicin is recommended.",
