      "correct": "D",
      "answerText": "(D) Window period/Gap period",
      "explanation": "In this serological profile:\nHBsAg = Negative\nAnti-HBs = Negative\nHBeAg = Negative\nAnti-HBe = Negative\nTotal anti-HBc = Positive\nThe key finding is isolated anti-HBc positivity. During the natural course of acute hepatitis B infection, there is a phase called the window period. In this period, HBsAg has disappeared, but anti-HBs has not yet appeared. Therefore, both HBsAg and anti-HBs are negative.\nThe only detectable marker during this time is anti-HBc, particularly IgM anti-HBc, which indicates recent infection. Total anti-HBc includes both IgM and IgG antibodies, so its positivity supports exposure to hepatitis B virus.\nChronic hepatitis B would show persistent HBsAg positivity. An inactive carrier state also requires HBsAg positivity. Remote past infection would typically show anti-HBs positivity along with anti-HBc.\nSince HBsAg and anti-HBs are both negative but anti-HBc is positive, the most appropriate diagnosis is the window (gap) period of acute hepatitis B infection.",
