      "explanation": "Breast conservation surgery (BCS), also called lumpectomy or wide local excision, is performed in early-stage breast cancer to remove the tumor along with a margin of normal surrounding tissue while preserving most of the breast. The primary goal is to achieve oncological safety with good cosmetic outcome.\nA key component of BCS is wide local excision, making option (A) correct. After surgery, post-operative radiotherapy is routinely given to the remaining breast tissue to reduce the risk of local recurrence; therefore, option (B) is also a standard part of treatment.\nEvaluation of lymph node status is essential in breast cancer management. However, in early-stage disease, this is typically done through sentinel lymph node biopsy, which is less invasive than full axillary clearance. Hence, some form of limited lymph node assessment (option C) is usually included.\nAxillary dissection, which involves removal of multiple lymph nodes from the axilla, is not routinely performed in breast conservation surgery unless there is proven nodal involvement. Therefore, option (D) does not accurately describe a standard aspect of breast conservation surgery.",
