      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Contaminated",
      "explanation": "Open cardiac massage is performed during emergency thoracotomy, usually in cases of severe trauma or cardiac arrest. Although the procedure involves entering the thoracic cavity under surgical conditions, it is typically done in an emergency setting rather than a planned sterile operating room procedure. Because of the urgent nature and possible exposure to non-sterile environments, the wound is classified as contaminated.\nAccording to surgical wound classification, wounds are divided into clean, clean-contaminated, contaminated, and dirty. A clean wound involves no entry into respiratory, gastrointestinal, or genitourinary tracts and is performed under ideal sterile conditions. In open cardiac massage, the chest cavity is opened emergently, often after trauma, and there is a high risk of bacterial contamination from the environment or injured tissues.\nTherefore, due to the emergency nature of the procedure and increased risk of contamination, open cardiac massage is categorized as a contaminated wound.",
