      "number": 142,
      "printedNumber": 142,
      "paperSession": "May 2023",
      "statement": "A 2-month-old pediatric patient has had a persistent swelling on the head since birth. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
      "options": [
        "Cephalhematoma",
        "Caput succedaneum",
        "Soft tissue swelling",
        "None of the above"
      ],
      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Cephalhematoma",
      "explanation": "The key differentiating feature in this clinical scenario is the persistence of the head swelling for two months after birth. A caput succedaneum is a diffuse, edematous swelling of the scalp that is present at birth. It is caused by pressure on the presenting part of the scalp during delivery, crosses suture lines, and characteristically resolves within the first few days of life. In contrast, a cephalhematoma is a subperiosteal collection of blood caused by the rupture of blood vessels between the skull and the periosteum. It is sharply demarcated, does not cross suture lines, and, crucially, resolves much more slowly. A cephalhematoma can take several weeks to months to completely resolve and may undergo calcification. Therefore, a head swelling that is still present at 2 months of age is almost certainly a cephalhematoma, not a caput succedaneum.",
