      "paperSession": "Nov 2023",
      "statement": "A child arrives with acute respiratory distress, and examination shows a scaphoid abdomen. Which of the following interventions should be avoided in this patient?",
      "options": [
        "Positive Pressure Ventilation (PPV)",
        "Bag and mask ventilation",
        "Oxygen therapy",
        "Intubation"
      ],
      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Bag and mask ventilation",
      "explanation": "The clinical picture of a newborn in respiratory distress with a scaphoid (sunken) abdomen is highly suggestive of a congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH). In CDH, abdominal organs herniate into the chest cavity, compressing the developing lung and causing pulmonary hypoplasia. In this situation, bag and mask ventilation is contraindicated. This is because it forces air not only into the lungs but also into the stomach and intestines. The air insufflated into the herniated abdominal viscera will cause them to distend further, increasing the compression on the already hypoplastic lung, worsening the respiratory distress, and compromising cardiovascular function. The immediate management requires endotracheal intubation to secure the airway and provide controlled positive pressure ventilation directly to the lungs, bypassing the gastrointestinal tract. Oxygen therapy is also essential.",
