      "answerText": "(B) 30%",
      "explanation": "In oxygen therapy using a nasal cannula, the delivered FiO₂ increases approximately 4% for every 1 L/min increase in oxygen flow, starting from room air.\nRoom air contains about 21% oxygen. The approximate formula used clinically is:\nFiO₂ ≈ 21% + (4 × flow rate in L/min)\nFor a flow rate of 3 L/min:\nFiO₂ ≈ 21% + (4 × 3)\nFiO₂ ≈ 21% + 12%\nFiO₂ ≈ 33%\nHowever, because nasal cannula provides variable FiO₂ (depending on the patient’s tidal volume, respiratory rate, and inspiratory flow), the delivered oxygen concentration is usually approximated.\nClinically accepted values are:\n1 L/min → 24%\n2 L/min → 28%\n3 L/min → 30%\n4 L/min → 36%\n5 L/min → 40%\nThus, at 3 L/min, the commonly accepted delivered FiO₂ is approximately 30%, making option (B) correct.",
