      "explanation": "Perinatal (vertical) transmission rates vary among infections:\n1. CMV is the most common congenital viral infection. Primary maternal infection has a transmission rate of about 30–40%, and even higher in later pregnancy.\n2. HIV has a transmission rate of 15–45% without treatment, but this can be reduced to <5% with antiretroviral therapy.\n3. HSV has a high transmission risk (30–50%) if the mother has a primary genital infection near delivery.\nIn contrast, rubella has a lower overall rate of perinatal transmission because:\n4. Transmission mainly occurs during primary maternal infection in early pregnancy.\n5. The risk decreases significantly in later trimesters.\n6. Widespread vaccination programs have dramatically reduced the incidence of maternal rubella infection, making congenital rubella syndrome relatively rare in vaccinated populations.\nAlthough early pregnancy rubella infection can have severe fetal consequences, the overall occurrence of perinatal transmission is lower compared to CMV and untreated HIV.\nTherefore, among the given options, the condition with the lowest occurrence of perinatal transmission is Rubella.",
