      "explanation": "Perinatal transmission refers to infections that are passed from mother to child during pregnancy (transplacental), during labor and delivery, or shortly after birth. Several infectious agents are well known to be transmitted in this period.\nCytomegalovirus (CMV) is one of the most common congenital infections. It is transmitted transplacentally and can also be acquired during delivery or through breast milk. Rubella virus is transmitted transplacentally when a mother acquires primary infection during pregnancy, especially in the first trimester, leading to congenital rubella syndrome. Hepatitis B virus is commonly transmitted during childbirth from an infected mother to her newborn, particularly if the mother is HBsAg positive.\nIn contrast, meningitis is not a specific infectious agent but a clinical condition characterized by inflammation of the meninges. While certain organisms causing neonatal meningitis (such as Group B Streptococcus) may be transmitted during birth, meningitis itself is not transmitted as a disease entity.\nTherefore, among the options given, meningitis is not transmitted during the perinatal period.",
