      "answerText": "(A) Hepatitis A.",
      "explanation": "Perinatal transmission refers to infection passed from mother to infant during pregnancy, at the time of delivery, or shortly after birth. The likelihood of vertical transmission varies among infectious agents.\n1. Cytomegalovirus (CMV) is one of the most common congenital infections. It is frequently transmitted transplacentally, especially during primary maternal infection, with a transmission rate of around 30–40%.\n2. Herpes simplex virus (HSV) can also be transmitted perinatally, particularly during vaginal delivery if the mother has an active genital lesion. In primary maternal infection near term, the transmission risk may reach 30–50%.\n3. Rubella is transmitted transplacentally when maternal infection occurs during pregnancy, especially in the first trimester, with a very high fetal transmission rate.\nIn contrast, Hepatitis A is primarily transmitted through the fecal–oral route. Vertical or perinatal transmission of Hepatitis A is extremely rare. Although maternal infection may occur during pregnancy, it seldom leads to congenital infection.\nTherefore, among the given options, Hepatitis A has the lowest possibility of perinatal transmission.",
