      "explanation": "A patient with multiple prior cesarean sections who develops a placenta accreta spectrum (PAS) disorder is at extremely high risk for severe maternal morbidity. Counseling must be comprehensive. The main risks include:\n1 Massive obstetric hemorrhage (excessive bleeding): This is the most significant and life-threatening risk.\n2 Cesarean hysterectomy (chances of hysterectomy): This is often the planned and necessary management to control bleeding.\n3 Injury to adjacent organs: Because the placenta often invades through the uterine wall anteriorly into the bladder, the risk of bladder injury is very high and a specific, expected complication.\nWhile bowel injury is a potential risk in any complex abdominal surgery, it is less of a direct and specific complication of PAS itself compared to bladder injury. The placenta in PAS is typically located anteriorly over the old C-section scar, directly adjacent to the bladder, whereas the bowel is located more posteriorly. Therefore, while mentioned, it would be less emphasized than the near certainty of bleeding and high risk of bladder injury and hysterectomy.",
