      "answerText": "(C) Repeat ICT after 4 weeks",
      "explanation": "In an Rh-negative pregnant woman, the Indirect Coombs Test (ICT) is used to detect circulating anti-D antibodies and assess the risk of hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn (HDFN). A titer of 1:4 at 28 weeks is considered a low (non-critical) antibody titer.\nManagement depends on whether the antibody level has reached the critical titer, usually 1:16 (or 1:32 in some centers). Titers below the critical value indicate that significant fetal hemolysis is unlikely at that time.\nSince the current titer is only 1:4:\n1. There is no immediate indication for MCA Doppler, which is done when titers reach critical levels.\n2. Anti-D immunoglobulin is not given once the mother is already sensitized (positive ICT).\n3. Doing nothing is incorrect because monitoring is required.\nTherefore, the appropriate next step is serial monitoring of antibody titers, typically every 4 weeks until 28–32 weeks, then more frequently if needed.\nHence, the correct management is to repeat the ICT after 4 weeks.",
