      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Langer's lines",
      "explanation": "The image shows characteristic linear patterns over the face and body that correspond to the natural orientation of collagen fibers within the dermis. These are known as Langer’s lines, also called skin tension lines. They represent the direction of maximum skin tension and are clinically important in surgical practice.\nIncisions made parallel to Langer’s lines tend to heal better and produce less noticeable scars because they follow the natural alignment of collagen fibers. In contrast, incisions made perpendicular to these lines are more likely to gape and result in wider scars.\nBlaschko’s lines represent embryologic cell migration patterns and are typically invisible unless highlighted by certain skin disorders. Dermatomes represent areas of skin supplied by specific spinal nerves, and Hinderer’s lines are used in cosmetic facial surgery.\nThe patterns shown in the image are continuous, curved, and symmetrical over the body and face, consistent with the classic depiction of Langer’s lines. Therefore, the structure identified in the image is Langer’s lines.",
