      "paperSession": "May 2023",
      "statement": "What is the most appropriate immediate response when a patient’s blood pressure suddenly falls to 40 mmHg during squint surgery?",
      "options": [
        "Give atropine",
        "Increase level of anesthesia",
        "Ask the surgeon to stop the surgery",
        "Give adrenaline"
      ],
      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) Ask the surgeon to stop the surgery",
      "explanation": "During squint (strabismus) surgery, traction on the extraocular muscles—especially the medial rectus—can trigger the oculocardiac reflex (OCR). This is a trigemino-vagal reflex in which stimulation of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve leads to increased vagal tone via the vagus nerve. The result can be bradycardia, hypotension, arrhythmias, or even asystole.\nIf the patient’s blood pressure suddenly falls to 40 mmHg intraoperatively, the most immediate and crucial step is to stop the surgical stimulus. Removing the traction on the extraocular muscle often reverses the reflex rapidly.\nAfter stopping the stimulus:\n1. Ensure adequate oxygenation.\n2. Deepen anesthesia if required.\n3. Administer atropine if bradycardia persists.\nAdrenaline is not the first-line response unless there is cardiac arrest or severe unresponsive hypotension.\nTherefore, the most appropriate immediate action when sudden hypotension occurs during squint surgery is to ask the surgeon to stop the surgery.",
      "pages": [
        249,
        286
      ],
      "difficulty": "Medium",
