      "answerText": "(C) Metabolic acidosis",
      "explanation": "The serum anion gap (AG) is a calculated value used to help determine the cause of metabolic acidosis. It is calculated as: AG = [Na⁺] - ([Cl⁻] + [HCO₃⁻]). This value represents the concentration of unmeasured anions (e.g., albumin, phosphate, sulfate) in the plasma. Metabolic acidosis can be broadly divided into two types based on the anion gap. A high anion gap metabolic acidosis occurs when there is an increase in unmeasured anions due to the accumulation of acids other than hydrochloric acid. Examples include lactic acidosis, ketoacidosis, and ingestion of toxins like methanol or ethylene glycol. In these conditions, the added acid is buffered by bicarbonate (HCO₃), which decreases, and the unmeasured anion of the acid increases, thus widening the anion gap. In contrast, normal anion gap metabolic acidosis involves a loss of bicarbonate that is compensated by an increase in chloride, keeping the gap normal.",
