      "explanation": "2,3-Bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG), also known as 2,3-diphosphoglycerate (DPG), is a negative allosteric regulator of hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen. It plays a crucial role in adapting oxygen delivery to metabolic needs. 2,3-BPG binds to a specific site in the central cavity of the deoxygenated (T, or taut) hemoglobin tetramer. This binding pocket is formed by positively charged amino acid residues on the two beta-globin chains. The binding of the negatively charged 2,3-BPG molecule stabilizes the T-state conformation of hemoglobin. This stabilization makes it energetically less favorable for hemoglobin to switch to the high-oxygen-affinity R (relaxed) state. As a result, the overall affinity of hemoglobin for oxygen is reduced, causing a rightward shift in the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve. This shift promotes the unloading and release of oxygen from hemoglobin into the peripheral tissues where oxygen tension is lower.",
