      "answerText": "(C) Methemoglobinemia",
      "explanation": "The combination of symptoms suggesting severe tissue hypoxia (fatigue, prostration, exacerbated angina) and the classic finding of \"chocolate-brown\" blood is pathognomonic for methemoglobinemia. In this condition, the iron atom in the heme group of hemoglobin is oxidized from the ferrous (Fe²⁺) state to the ferric (Fe³⁺) state. Hemoglobin in the ferric state, known as methemoglobin, is incapable of binding and transporting oxygen. This results in a functional anemia and impaired oxygen delivery to the tissues. Patients with angina may be taking nitrates, which are known oxidizing agents that can induce methemoglobinemia, providing a potential link in this case. Carboxyhemoglobinemia (A) from carbon monoxide poisoning causes a cherry-red blood color. Sideroblastic anemia (B) does not alter blood color in this way. Sulfhemoglobinemia (D) causes a greenish-black discoloration and is much rarer.",
