      "number": 35,
      "printedNumber": 35,
      "paperSession": "May 2022",
      "statement": "\"Cell in cell\" appearance is seen in which of the following?",
      "options": [
        "Necrosis",
        "Apoptosis",
        "Necroptosis",
        "Entosis"
      ],
      "correct": "D",
      "answerText": "(D) Entosis",
      "explanation": "The \"cell-in-cell\" structure describes the presence of one or more cells within the cytoplasm of another cell. While this can be seen when a phagocyte engulfs an apoptotic body (emperipolesis), the term is most specifically associated with entosis. Entosis is a distinct biological process where one living epithelial cell actively invades and is engulfed by a neighboring cell. The internalized \"guest\" cell remains alive for some time within a vacuole inside the \"host\" cell. The fate of the internalized cell can vary: it may be degraded, it may be released, or it may undergo cell division within the host. This phenomenon is distinct from phagocytosis of dead cells. Entosis is often observed in tumors and is thought to be a mechanism of cell competition. Necrosis (A) and necroptosis (C) are forms of lytic cell death and do not involve cell engulfment.",
      "pages": [
        382,
        408
      ],
      "difficulty": "Medium",
