      "answerText": "(B) 25 mmHg",
      "explanation": "The question is slightly ambiguous but most likely refers to the plasma colloid osmotic (oncotic) pressure, which is a major determinant of fluid movement between the capillaries and the interstitial tissue. This pressure is generated by plasma proteins, primarily albumin, which are too large to pass freely across the capillary wall. This protein concentration creates an osmotic force that pulls fluid from the interstitial space back into the capillaries. The normal value for plasma oncotic pressure is approximately 25 mmHg. The oncotic pressure of the interstitial fluid itself is much lower (around 8 mmHg) due to the low protein concentration in the tissue space. In the context of the Starling forces that govern tissue fluid balance, 25 mmHg is the standard value representing the inward-pulling force of the plasma. The other values are outside the normal physiological range for this parameter.",
