      "answerText": "(C) Hesitation cuts",
      "explanation": "The image displays multiple superficial, parallel incised wounds located at a typical site for self-harm, such as the wrist. These are known as hesitation cuts or tentative injuries. They are a classic finding in cases of suicide involving sharp force injury. These cuts represent the person's indecision or attempts to test the sharpness of the instrument or the pain level before making the final, deeper, and fatal wound. They are typically found adjacent to the main lethal wound. Their presence strongly suggests that the manner of death was suicide, as they are rarely seen in homicidal sharp force injuries, where the attack is typically more determined and forceful from the outset. A laceration (A) is a tear caused by blunt force. A patterned bruise (B) is a contusion showing the shape of the object that caused it. An abrasion (D) is a scrape.",
