      "number": 275,
      "printedNumber": 75,
      "paperSession": "Nov 2022",
      "statement": "Which of the following is not a cardiac poison?",
      "options": [
        "Atropa belladonna",
        "Aconite",
        "Cerbera thevetia",
        "Nicotiana tabacum"
      ],
      "correct": "D",
      "answerText": "(D) Nicotiana tabacum",
      "explanation": "Aconite (Aconitum) and Cerbera thevetia (Yellow oleander) are well-known cardiac poisons.\nAconite contains aconitine, which acts on voltage-gated sodium channels and produces serious cardiac arrhythmias.\nCerbera thevetia contains cardiac glycosides (similar to digoxin) that directly affect the myocardium, leading to bradycardia, heart block, and fatal arrhythmias.\nAtropa belladonna contains atropine and other anticholinergic alkaloids. Although its primary action is anticholinergic (causing tachycardia, dry mouth, dilated pupils), severe poisoning can significantly affect cardiac rhythm. Hence, it is often grouped among poisons that can impact cardiac function.\nNicotiana tabacum (tobacco) contains nicotine, which primarily acts on nicotinic acetylcholine receptors affecting the autonomic nervous system. While it can influence heart rate and blood pressure, it is not classified as a primary cardiac poison like aconite or yellow oleander.\nTherefore, Nicotiana tabacum is not considered a classical cardiac poison.",
