      "explanation": "A ligature mark is a patterned abrasion or bruise produced by a cord, rope, wire, or similar material tied tightly around the neck. It is commonly seen in cases of hanging or ligature strangulation. The mark typically appears as a linear or circumferential abrasion encircling the neck and may show a distinct pattern corresponding to the material used.\nPressure abrasions are caused by compression of skin between two surfaces and usually appear as localized areas without a circumferential pattern. Graze abrasions are superficial injuries caused by friction against a rough surface and tend to be irregular and scraped in appearance. Imprint (patterned) abrasions reflect the design of the object causing injury but are not typically circumferential around the neck like a ligature mark.\nThus, based on the characteristic pattern around the neck, the injury depicted is most consistent with a ligature mark.",
