      "paperSession": "Nov 2022",
      "statement": "The hooch tragedy is related to which substance?",
      "options": [
        "Phosphorus",
        "Mercury",
        "Ethanol",
        "Methanol"
      ],
      "correct": "D",
      "answerText": "(D) Methanol",
      "explanation": "\"Hooch\" is a colloquial term for illicitly brewed alcoholic beverages. \"Hooch tragedies\" refer to mass poisoning events that occur when these beverages are consumed. These tragedies are almost always caused by contamination of the illicit liquor with methanol (methyl alcohol). Methanol is sometimes added to increase the apparent strength of the beverage or is produced as a byproduct of improper distillation. Unlike ethanol (the alcohol in regular drinks), methanol is highly toxic. It is metabolized by alcohol dehydrogenase in the liver to formaldehyde and then to formic acid. The accumulation of formic acid causes severe metabolic acidosis and is specifically toxic to the optic nerve, leading to the characteristic symptom of blindness. Other effects include abdominal pain, coma, and death.",
