      "explanation": "The patient's presentation with altered sensorium indicates severe malaria caused by P. falciparum. The WHO defines severe malaria by the presence of complications. Let's analyze the options based on these criteria:\n(A) Hypoglycemia (blood glucose <40 mg/dL) is a defining complication of severe malaria.\n(C) Acute kidney injury (serum creatinine >3 mg/dL) is also a criterion for severe malaria. A value of 5.2 mg/dL clearly meets this.\n(D) Cerebral malaria, defined by an unarousable coma, is a hallmark of severe disease.\n(B) Metabolic acidosis is a major complication, defined by a plasma bicarbonate <15 mmol/L or an arterial pH <7.35. An arterial pH > 7.2 is not in itself a complication; in fact, it is closer to the normal range than the severely low pH that would define this complication. A patient with severe metabolic acidosis would have a pH well below this level. Therefore, having a pH > 7.2 is NOT a defining complication.",
