      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) 1, 3 and 4",
      "explanation": "Vaccination strategies for the elderly focus on preventing diseases that are more common or severe in this age group.\n1. Varicella zoster virus vaccine: The recombinant zoster vaccine (Shingrix) is strongly recommended for adults aged 50 and older to prevent herpes zoster (shingles), a painful condition that increases in incidence and severity with age.\n2. Rota virus vaccine: This vaccine is part of the routine immunization schedule for infants to prevent severe gastroenteritis and is not recommended for adults.\n3. Influenza vaccine: Annual influenza vaccination is recommended for all adults, but it is especially important for the elderly, who are at high risk for severe complications from the flu.\n4. Pneumococcal vaccine: Vaccines against Streptococcus pneumoniae are recommended for older adults to protect against pneumonia, bacteremia, and meningitis.\nTherefore, the varicella-zoster, influenza, and pneumococcal vaccines are all advised for the elderly.",
