      "explanation": "The larva shown in the image has distinguishing morphological features typical of Anopheles mosquito larvae. Anopheles larvae are characterized by the absence of a siphon (respiratory tube). Because they lack a siphon, they lie parallel to the water surface, obtaining oxygen directly through spiracles located on the posterior abdominal segment.\nIn contrast:\n1. Culex and Aedes larvae possess a long respiratory siphon, which causes them to hang at an angle from the water surface.\n2. Mansonia larvae attach to aquatic plants to obtain oxygen and have a modified siphon.\nThe image shows a larva without a prominent siphon and with a relatively straight body posture, consistent with Anopheles. Additionally, Anopheles larvae have palmate hairs on abdominal segments, which help them float horizontally.\nSince septic tank water can serve as a breeding site and the larval morphology matches these features, the mosquito species present is : (A) Anopheles.",
