      "answerText": "(B) 1,2,4,5",
      "explanation": "Acid-fast staining (like the Ziehl-Neelsen stain) is a differential stain used to identify organisms that have a waxy mycolic acid layer in their cell walls, which resists decolorization by acid-alcohol.\n1 Nocardia species are bacteria that are filamentous and are characteristically weakly or partially acid-fast.\n2 Mycobacterium leprae, like all mycobacteria, is strongly acid-fast due to its high mycolic acid content.\n4 Cryptosporidium parvum and 5. Isospora belli are protozoan parasites whose oocysts have a cell wall that makes them acid-fast, which is a key feature for their identification in stool samples.\n3 Actinomyces is a gram-positive, filamentous bacterium, but it is not acid-fast. It is often confused with Nocardia morphologically, but the acid-fast stain is a crucial differentiating test. Therefore, organisms 1, 2, 4, and 5 are acid-fast.",
