      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Detection and quantification of DNA",
      "explanation": "Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is a molecular biology technique used to amplify specific segments of DNA. Standard PCR is a qualitative method used for the detection of a target DNA sequence. A variation of PCR, called Quantitative PCR or Real-Time PCR (qPCR), allows for the quantification of the amount of DNA present in a sample in real time. Therefore, PCR methods can be used for both detection and quantification of DNA. Statement (B) is partially correct in that RT-qPCR can detect and quantify mRNA, but it requires a preliminary reverse transcription step; the PCR part itself still works on DNA. Western blot (C) is a technique used to detect proteins, not nucleic acids. In Reverse Transcription PCR (RT-PCR), RNA is first converted into complementary DNA (cDNA), not recombinant DNA (rDNA) (D).",
