      "explanation": "Parvovirus B19 has a tropism for erythroid progenitor cells in the bone marrow and can cause a spectrum of diseases.\n(A) Correct. In patients with underlying hemolytic anemias like sickle cell disease, B19 infection can cause a transient but severe cessation of erythropoiesis, leading to an aplastic crisis.\n(B) Correct. If a non-immune pregnant woman is infected, the virus can cross the placenta, infect the fetus, cause severe anemia and heart failure, leading to hydrops fetalis and fetal death.\n(D) Correct. In adults, especially women, B19 infection is a common cause of acute, symmetrical, non-erosive polyarthritis, which can mimic rheumatoid arthritis or an SLE flare.\n(C) Incorrect. Erythema infectiosum, or \"fifth disease,\" is the classic \"slapped-cheek\" rash caused by B19. However, it is most common in school-aged children (typically 4-10 years old), not infants.",
