      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Tympanometry",
      "explanation": "Otoacoustic emissions (OAEs) are sounds generated by the outer hair cells of the cochlea, used for newborn hearing screening. A \"pass\" result indicates a healthy cochlea. A \"refer\" result (absent OAEs) can mean a problem in the cochlea (sensorineural hearing loss) OR a problem in the outer or middle ear that prevents the sound from getting in or the emission from getting out. The most common reason for a \"refer\" in a newborn is fluid in the middle ear (otitis media with effusion). Therefore, the immediate next step is to assess the middle ear status. Tympanometry is an objective test that measures the mobility of the eardrum and the pressure in the middle ear. It can quickly and reliably detect middle ear problems like fluid or eustachian tube dysfunction, helping to differentiate the cause of the absent OAEs.",
