      "explanation": "The Trendelenburg test assesses the function of the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus muscles, which are responsible for stabilizing the pelvis during walking. These muscles are innervated by the superior gluteal nerve.\nIn a normal person standing on the right leg, the right gluteus medius and minimus contract to keep the pelvis level. If these muscles are weak or paralyzed, the pelvis drops on the opposite (left) side. This is a positive Trendelenburg sign.\nA positive Trendelenburg test on the right side means that when the patient stands on the right leg, the pelvis drops on the left side. This indicates weakness of the right gluteus medius and minimus, and therefore injury to the right superior gluteal nerve.\nThe inferior gluteal nerve supplies the gluteus maximus, which is involved in hip extension, not pelvic stabilization. Hence, it is not responsible for a positive Trendelenburg sign.\nTherefore, the involved nerve is the right superior gluteal nerve.",
