      "explanation": "This statement is incorrect. Rapid Sequence Intubation (RSI) is a specific technique for endotracheal intubation in patients who have a full stomach or are otherwise at high risk of aspiration and who still have intact airway reflexes. It involves the near-simultaneous administration of an induction agent and a neuromuscular blocking agent. Patients in cardiac arrest are, by definition, unconscious and flaccid; they have no muscle tone and no protective airway reflexes. Therefore, there is no need to administer anesthetic or paralytic drugs to facilitate intubation. Airway management in cardiac arrest prioritizes chest compressions and involves basic maneuvers followed by intubation without drugs. The other statements are correct components of RSI: preoxygenation (A) is vital, high-volume, low-pressure cuffs (B) are standard for modern endotracheal tubes, and the co-administration of induction and paralytic agents (D) is the defining feature of the technique.",
