      "explanation": "The patient has severe ARDS, evidenced by refractory hypoxemia (PaO2 of 50 mmHg on 90% FiO2). The goal is to improve oxygenation while minimizing ventilator-induced lung injury. Her tidal volume is 6 ml/kg (360/60), which is appropriate for a lung-protective strategy. Her PaCO2 and pH are normal, so her ventilation is adequate; there is no need to change the respiratory rate (D). Reducing FiO2 (B) would worsen the life-threatening hypoxemia. Increasing the tidal volume (C) would violate the lung-protective strategy. The most appropriate intervention is to increase the PEEP. In ARDS, the lungs are stiff and prone to alveolar collapse (atelectasis). Increasing PEEP helps to recruit collapsed alveoli, keep them open at the end of expiration, improve ventilation-perfusion matching, and thereby enhance oxygenation. This allows for a subsequent reduction in the FiO2 to less toxic levels (<60%).",
