      "paperSession": "May 2022",
      "statement": "Hamman sign is seen in which of the following conditions?",
      "options": [
        "Pneumomediastinum",
        "Pneumoperitoneum",
        "Pneumopericardium",
        "Hydropneumothorax"
      ],
      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Pneumomediastinum",
      "explanation": "Hamman's sign, also known as Hamman's crunch, is a distinctive and pathognomonic clinical finding for pneumomediastinum (the presence of gas or air in the mediastinum). It is described as a crunching, crackling, or bubbling sound heard over the precordium that is synchronous with the heartbeat. The sound is produced by the heart beating against the air-filled mediastinal tissues. It is often best heard when the patient is in the left lateral decubitus position. Pneumomediastinum can result from various causes, including alveolar rupture (e.g., from severe coughing or Valsalva), esophageal perforation (Boerhaave syndrome), or trauma. While sometimes associated with pneumothorax or pneumopericardium, the sign itself localizes the air to the mediastinum.",
