      "correct": "D",
      "answerText": "(D) Subdural hematoma",
      "explanation": "The combination of head trauma (even a seemingly minor fall) in an elderly person followed by the gradual onset of headache and neurological symptoms (such as confusion, lethargy, or focal deficits) over days to weeks is the classic presentation of a chronic subdural hematoma (SDH). Elderly individuals are predisposed to this condition because age-related brain atrophy stretches the bridging veins that cross the subdural space. A minor trauma can tear these veins, leading to a slow, low-pressure venous bleed. The hematoma gradually expands, causing symptoms as it exerts mass effect on the brain. The other options are not consistent with this acute-on-chronic presentation following trauma. A chronic SDH is a critical diagnosis to consider in any elderly patient with a recent fall and new neurological complaints.",
