      "correct": "D",
      "answerText": "(D) 1,2,3,4,5 and 6",
      "explanation": "The correct answer is (D) 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6.\nPancreatic necrosis is a severe complication of acute pancreatitis and requires comprehensive management.\n1. IV antibiotics are indicated if infected pancreatic necrosis is suspected or confirmed. Broad-spectrum antibiotics covering gut flora are used.\n2. IV fluids are essential in all cases to maintain hemodynamic stability and prevent organ failure. Aggressive fluid resuscitation is a cornerstone of early management.\n3. Culture of aspirated fluid helps confirm infection and guides antibiotic therapy. Fine needle aspiration may be done if infection is suspected.\n4. MRI (or contrast-enhanced CT) helps assess the extent of necrosis and complications such as fluid collections or abscess formation.\n5. Assisted drainage (percutaneous or endoscopic) is used for infected collections or symptomatic fluid accumulation.\n6. Laparotomy (surgical necrosectomy) may be required in cases not responding to minimally invasive management or in extensive infected necrosis.\nThus, management of pancreatic necrosis may include all the listed measures, making option (D) correct.",
