      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) >15%",
      "explanation": "Under the National Tuberculosis Elimination Program (NTEP), districts are stratified based on the burden of HIV–TB co-infection to prioritize intensified collaborative TB–HIV activities. A district is labeled as a high-priority district when the prevalence of HIV among TB patients exceeds 15%.\nThis classification helps in strengthening integrated TB–HIV services such as:\n1. Routine HIV testing for all TB patients\n2. Early initiation of antiretroviral therapy (ART)\n3. Cotrimoxazole preventive therapy (CPT)\n4. Bidirectional screening (TB screening in PLHIV and HIV testing in TB cases)\nThe rationale behind this threshold is that HIV significantly increases the risk of progression from latent TB infection to active TB disease. Therefore, districts with HIV–TB co-infection rates above 15% require enhanced surveillance, intensified case finding, and closer programmatic monitoring.\nThus, according to NTEP guidelines, the criterion for designating a district as high priority based on HIV–TB co-infection prevalence is greater than 15%.",
