      "explanation": "Central cyanosis is a bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes (especially the tongue and lips) caused by an increased concentration of deoxygenated hemoglobin (>5 g/dL) in the arterial blood. It results from inadequate oxygenation of blood in the lungs or from right-to-left shunting of blood. High altitude (C) causes hypoxemia and central cyanosis. A pulmonary arteriovenous fistula (A) is a right-to-left shunt that allows deoxygenated blood to bypass the pulmonary capillaries, causing central cyanosis. Methemoglobinemia (D) is a condition where iron in hemoglobin is oxidized to the ferric (Fe³⁺) state, which cannot bind oxygen, leading to functional anemia and central cyanosis. Hypothermia (B) typically causes peripheral cyanosis. Severe cold causes intense peripheral vasoconstriction, slowing blood flow in the extremities. This allows more time for oxygen extraction by tissues, increasing deoxygenated hemoglobin in the peripheral capillary beds, but arterial oxygen saturation remains normal.",
