      "explanation": "On the ECG strip, there are visible P waves that are not followed by QRS complexes, indicating intermittent failure of atrioventricular (AV) conduction. Some atrial impulses are conducted to the ventricles, while others are blocked. This pattern is characteristic of a second-degree AV block.\n1. In first-degree heart block, every P wave is followed by a QRS complex, but the PR interval is prolonged.\n2. In third-degree (complete) heart block, there is complete AV dissociation, with no relationship between P waves and QRS complexes.\n3. Ventricular tachycardia would show rapid, wide QRS complexes without normal preceding P waves.\n4. The ECG shown demonstrates dropped beats with intermittent conduction, consistent with second-degree AV block (likely Mobitz type I or II depending on PR behavior).\nThus, the conduction abnormality indicated is second-degree heart block.",
