      "explanation": "Cyanotic congenital heart diseases can be broadly classified based on their effect on pulmonary blood flow (PBF).\n1. Tetralogy of Fallot (2) is the classic example of cyanotic heart disease with decreased PBF due to right ventricular outflow tract obstruction (pulmonary stenosis).\n2. Ebstein anomaly (1) typically presents with variable cyanosis and decreased PBF due to severe tricuspid regurgitation and right-to-left shunting at the atrial level.\n3. Transposition of the Great Arteries (TGA) (3) involves parallel circulations. For the infant to survive, there must be mixing between the two circuits (e.g., via a PDA, VSD, or ASD). The pulmonary circulation arises from the low-pressure left ventricle, resulting in increased PBF.\n4. Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Communication (TAPVC) (4) involves all four pulmonary veins draining into the systemic venous circulation instead of the left atrium. This mixed blood must then shunt to the left side of the heart. The entire cardiac output passes through the pulmonary circulation, leading to significantly increased PBF.",
