      "explanation": "Skin grafts undergo two distinct types of contraction.\nPrimary contracture is the immediate elastic recoil of the skin graft upon harvesting. This is proportional to the amount of dermis in the graft. Since full-thickness grafts contain the entire dermis, they have significantly more primary contracture (3 is correct, 1 is incorrect).\nSecondary contracture is the shrinkage of the graft after it has been placed on the wound bed, driven by myofibroblasts. The dermis provides a scaffold that resists this contraction. Since split-thickness grafts have less dermis, they are less able to resist this force and undergo more secondary contracture (2 is correct, 4 is incorrect).\nThe rule of thumb is: the thicker the graft (full-thickness), the more primary contraction and the less secondary contraction.",
