      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Ripstein's procedure",
      "explanation": "The image depicts rectal prolapse, characterized by a full-thickness protrusion of the rectum through the anal canal with visible concentric mucosal folds. Management of rectal prolapse includes both abdominal and perineal procedures: (B)\tDelorme’s operation – A perineal procedure used for mucosal or short-segment prolapse. (C)\tThiersch operation – Involves placing a synthetic or wire encirclement around the anus to prevent prolapse, commonly used in high-risk patients. (D)\tAltemeier’s procedure – A perineal rectosigmoidectomy used for full-thickness prolapse. Ripstein’s procedure (A), however, is an abdominal rectopexy involving placement of a mesh to fix the rectum to the sacrum. It is not a perineal procedure and is less commonly performed today due to complications such as constipation. Since the question asks which procedure is not used to manage the depicted perineal condition (i.e., not a perineal approach), the correct answer is Ripstein’s procedure.",
