      "explanation": "Non-operative management (NOM) is the standard of care for hemodynamically stable patients with blunt solid organ injuries, such as a grade 3 splenic trauma. This approach involves intensive monitoring for signs of failure, which would necessitate a shift to surgical intervention. While a significant drop in hemoglobin requiring multiple transfusions can indicate failure of NOM, the finding of pneumoperitoneum (free air in the peritoneal cavity) on imaging is an absolute indication for an immediate exploratory laparotomy. Pneumoperitoneum is a cardinal sign of a perforated hollow viscus (e.g., stomach, bowel). Its presence indicates a missed intra-abdominal injury that is a surgical emergency, independent of the splenic injury's status. Continuing with NOM in the presence of a bowel perforation would lead to life-threatening peritonitis and sepsis. Therefore, the discovery of pneumoperitoneum mandates an urgent surgical exploration.",
