      "explanation": "Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (CTEV), or clubfoot, is a complex congenital deformity of the foot involving multiple joints and soft tissues. The name itself describes the components of the deformity. The acronym CAVE is often used to remember the four distinct components :\n1. Cavus : An exaggerated high arch of the foot, caused by flexion of the forefoot on the hindfoot.\n2. Adductus : The forefoot is deviated medially (adduction) at the midtarsal joint.\n3. Varus : The heel is turned inward (inversion) at the subtalar joint. This is often referred to instead of inversion, though the motion is the same.\n4. Equinus : The entire foot is plantarflexed at the ankle joint, pointing downwards like a horse's hoof.\nTherefore, the correct combination of deformities is equinus, inversion (varus), forefoot adduction, and cavus.",
