      "explanation": "Cerclage procedures are performed to treat cervical incompetence, which can lead to recurrent mid-trimester pregnancy losses or preterm birth. The timing of the procedure is important.\nBenson and Durfee cerclage is a transabdominal cerclage procedure. It is usually performed during pregnancy (often in the late first trimester or early second trimester) when transvaginal cerclage is not feasible or has previously failed. It may also be done pre-pregnancy, but it is specifically known as an abdominal cerclage that can be performed in pregnant women.\nIn contrast:\n1. Lash and Lash cerclage is done before pregnancy (interval cerclage) and is not typically performed during pregnancy.\n2. Wurm’s cerclage is not a standard procedure used for cervical incompetence.\n3. Laparoscopic cerclage can be performed, but it is usually done as an interval procedure before conception, though it may also be done early in pregnancy in selected cases.\nTherefore, among the options provided, the cerclage procedure performed in a pregnant female is Benson and Durfee cerclage.",
