      "explanation": "Vulvar cancer is a relatively rare gynecological malignancy that predominantly affects older, postmenopausal women. The incidence of vulvar cancer increases significantly with age. There are two main etiological pathways. The first is related to chronic infection with high-risk human papillomavirus (HPV), which typically occurs in younger women. The second, and more common, pathway is HPV-independent and is associated with chronic inflammatory skin conditions of the vulva, such as lichen sclerosus. This second type accounts for the majority of cases and is responsible for the peak incidence of the disease in the postmenopausal period. The median age at diagnosis for vulvar cancer is approximately 68-70 years, with the majority of cases occurring in women between the ages of 55 and 85 years. It is uncommon in women under the age of 50.",
