      "explanation": "Alopecias are classified into two major groups : non-scarring (non-cicatricial) and scarring (cicatricial). In non-scarring alopecias, the underlying hair follicle structure remains intact, meaning there is potential for hair regrowth. This is the more common category and includes :\n1. Alopecia areata, an autoimmune condition causing patchy hair loss.\n2. Telogen effluvium, a diffuse shedding of resting hairs after a physiological stressor.\n3. Androgenic alopecia, the common male and female pattern hair loss.\n4. In contrast, scarring alopecias involve inflammation-mediated destruction of the hair follicle stem cells, leading to irreversible hair loss and replacement by fibrous scar tissue. Frontal fibrosing alopecia is a primary scarring alopecia, considered a clinical variant of lichen planopilaris. Therefore, the first three options are all types of non-scarring alopecia.",
