      "explanation": "Before initiating multidrug therapy (MDT) for leprosy, the diagnosis is made clinically based on the presence of at least one of the cardinal signs of leprosy as per WHO guidelines.\nThe three cardinal signs are:\n1 Hypopigmented or erythematous skin lesion with definite sensory loss\n2 Thickened peripheral nerve with sensory and/or motor impairment\n3 Demonstration of acid-fast bacilli in a slit-skin smear\nStatement 1 is correct because a hypopigmented patch with definite sensory loss is a cardinal sign.\nStatement 3 is correct because nerve thickening must be accompanied by sensory or motor deficit to be diagnostic.\nStatement 2 (nerve thickening alone) is insufficient unless associated with functional impairment.\nStatement 4 (skin biopsy demonstrating bacilli) is not mandatory before starting MDT; treatment is usually initiated on clinical grounds without waiting for biopsy confirmation.\nThus, MDT can be started when criteria 1 and 3 are met, making option (B) correct.",
