      "number": 387,
      "printedNumber": 187,
      "paperSession": "Nov 2022",
      "statement": "During squint surgery, the anesthesiologist notices a sudden drop in blood pressure to 40 mmHg. What should be the most appropriate immediate response?",
      "options": [
        "Give atropine",
        "Increase level of anesthesia",
        "Ask the surgeon to stop the surgery",
        "Give adrenaline"
      ],
      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) Ask the surgeon to stop the surgery",
      "explanation": "During squint (strabismus) surgery, traction on the extraocular muscles—especially the medial rectus—can trigger the oculocardiac reflex (OCR). This reflex is mediated by the trigeminal nerve (afferent limb) and the vagus nerve (efferent limb), leading to sudden bradycardia, hypotension, arrhythmias, or even asystole.\n1. A sudden drop in blood pressure to 40 mmHg strongly suggests a severe vagal response due to this reflex.\n2. The first and most important immediate step is to ask the surgeon to stop traction on the muscle, thereby removing the triggering stimulus. Often, this alone leads to rapid recovery of heart rate and blood pressure.\n3. If bradycardia or hypotension persists after stopping stimulation, atropine may then be administered. Increasing anesthesia is not the first response, and adrenaline is reserved for refractory severe cases.\nThus, the most appropriate immediate action is to stop the surgical stimulus, making option (C) correct.",
      "pages": [
        476,
        521
      ],
      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "difficultyEstimated": true,
      "topic": "Anesthesia"
