      "explanation": "The HER2/neu status of a breast cancer is a critical biomarker for determining eligibility for anti-HER2 targeted therapies like trastuzumab. The initial assessment is typically done by immunohistochemistry (IHC), which stains for the HER2 protein on the surface of tumor cells. The result is scored from 0 to 3+.\nA score of 0 or 1+ is considered HER2 negative.\nA score of 3+ (strong, complete membrane staining in >10% of tumor cells) is considered HER2 positive.\nA score of 2+ is considered equivocal or borderline.\nAccording to ASCO/CAP guidelines, when the IHC result is equivocal (2+), reflex testing with Fluorescence In-Situ Hybridization (FISH) is required to make a definitive determination. FISH is a molecular test that directly counts the number of copies of the HER2 gene within the tumor cell nuclei to assess for gene amplification. A positive FISH result will then classify the 2+ case as HER2 positive.",
