      "explanation": "In ARDS caused by SARS-CoV-2 infection, the primary pathological process is diffuse alveolar damage (DAD). The virus infects type II pneumocytes, leading to inflammation, cytokine release, and increased vascular permeability. This results in edema, hyaline membrane formation, and thickening of the alveolar wall.\nIn the given diagram, label A represents the alveolar septum (alveolar wall), which contains capillaries and interstitial tissue. During ARDS, this septal layer becomes edematous, inflamed, and thickened, appearing enlarged due to fluid accumulation and inflammatory cell infiltration.\nThe thickened alveolar septum increases the diffusion distance for oxygen, leading to severe hypoxemia — the hallmark of ARDS.\nLabels B and D correspond to surface lining or epithelial components, but the major enlargement and structural thickening in ARDS occur in the inter alveolar septum, not just the epithelial surface.\nTherefore, the layer that becomes enlarged and involved due to SARS-CoV-2 infection is A (Option B).",
