      "answerText": "(B) Ferritin translation is decreased by binding IRP to IRE",
      "explanation": "In a state of iron deficiency, intracellular iron levels are low. This causes the iron regulatory protein (IRP) to be active and bind to specific stem-loop structures on mRNAs called iron-responsive elements (IREs). The location of the IRE determines the effect of IRP binding.\nThe IRE for ferritin (the iron storage protein) is located in the 5' untranslated region (5' UTR) of its mRNA. When IRP binds here, it sterically hinders the assembly of the ribosomal complex, thereby blocking and decreasing the translation of ferritin. This makes physiological sense, as the cell does not want to store iron when it is deficient.\nThe IRE for the transferrin receptor 1 (which imports iron into the cell) is in the 3' UTR. IRP binding here stabilizes the mRNA and prevents its degradation, thus increasing its translation. This allows the cell to make more receptors to take up more iron.",
