      "answerText": "(D) Diuretic",
      "explanation": "Resistant hypertension is defined as blood pressure that remains above the target goal despite the concurrent use of three antihypertensive agents from different classes, taken at optimal doses. A crucial part of this definition is that one of the three agents must be a diuretic. Many cases of difficult-to-control hypertension are driven by volume expansion, making diuretics a cornerstone of therapy. Therefore, a patient cannot be diagnosed with true resistant hypertension unless their regimen includes a diuretic (typically a thiazide-like diuretic like chlorthalidone) and they are still not at goal. If a patient is on three non-diuretic medications, the first step is to add a diuretic before labeling them as resistant. This ensures that a major contributing factor (volume overload) has been addressed before proceeding to more complex investigations or fourth-line agents.",
