      "explanation": "Burking is a form of homicidal asphyxia in which the victim is killed by a combination of smothering and traumatic compression of the chest. Typically, the assailant sits on the victim’s chest to prevent respiratory movements while simultaneously covering the nose and mouth to block air entry. This dual mechanism causes rapid suffocation.\nThe term originates from the 19th-century criminal William Burke, who murdered victims without leaving obvious external injuries, making detection difficult. Because burking produces minimal visible marks, it may initially appear as a natural death unless carefully examined.\n(B) Option Smothering refers only to obstruction of the external air passages (nose and mouth) without chest compression.\n(C) Option Traumatic asphyxia results from severe chest compression, usually accidental (e.g., crowd crush), not intentional airway blockage.\n(D) Option Overlaying refers to accidental smothering of an infant by a sleeping adult.\nSince the scenario describes chest compression combined with blocking the nose and mouth, the correct term is Burking.",
