      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Affinity chromatography",
      "explanation": "Glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) reflects the average blood glucose level over the previous 2–3 months. It is formed by the non-enzymatic attachment of glucose to the N-terminal valine of the β-chain of hemoglobin A.\nAmong the available laboratory methods, boronate affinity chromatography is considered one of the most accurate and reliable techniques for measuring HbA1c. This method works by specifically binding glycated hemoglobin to a boronate resin, which has an affinity for the cis-diol groups of glycated proteins. Because it measures total glycated hemoglobin rather than relying on charge differences, it is less affected by hemoglobin variants and many analytical interferences.\nIon exchange chromatography separates hemoglobin fractions based on charge differences, but it may be affected by hemoglobinopathies. Immunoassays use antibodies directed against HbA1c but can be influenced by structural variants. Electrophoresis is mainly used for detecting hemoglobin variants rather than precise HbA1c quantification.\nTherefore, affinity chromatography provides the most accurate and specific measurement of HbA1c.",
