      "explanation": "Several tests are used to ensure the safety and quality of pasteurized milk.\nPhosphatase test (A) : This is the key test to verify the adequacy of pasteurization. The enzyme alkaline phosphatase is naturally present in milk and is destroyed at a temperature just slightly above that required for pasteurization. Therefore, the absence of this enzyme indicates that pasteurization was successful.\n(C) Standard plate count : This test measures the total number of viable bacteria remaining in the milk after pasteurization to ensure it meets public health standards.\n(D) Methylene blue reduction test : This is another test for microbial quality. Methylene blue is a dye that is decolorized by bacterial metabolism. The time it takes for the blue color to disappear is inversely proportional to the bacterial load.\n(B) Iodine test : This test is used to detect the presence of starch. It has no role in testing the quality or safety of milk.",
