      "answerText": "(D) RT-PCR followed by Real-time PCR",
      "explanation": "To perform a quantitative analysis of RNA, two steps are required. First, the RNA must be converted into a more stable complementary DNA (cDNA) molecule. This is achieved through Reverse Transcriptase PCR (RT-PCR) (B), which uses the enzyme reverse transcriptase. Second, this newly synthesized cDNA must be quantified. Real-time PCR (A), also known as quantitative PCR (qPCR), is the gold standard method for quantifying DNA. It monitors the amplification of a target DNA sequence in real-time by using fluorescent probes. The rate at which the fluorescence accumulates is proportional to the initial amount of the target sequence. Therefore, the most accurate and standard method for quantifying an RNA sample is to first perform reverse transcription and then quantify the resulting cDNA using real-time PCR. This two-step process is often collectively referred to as qRT-PCR or RT-qPCR.",
