      "answerText": "(B) 6",
      "explanation": "The Metabolic Equivalent of Task (MET) is a unit used to express the energy cost of physical activities. By definition, 1 MET is equivalent to the energy expenditure of sitting quietly, which corresponds to a baseline oxygen consumption rate of approximately 3.5 mL of oxygen per kilogram of body weight per minute. For an average 70 kg person, this equates to a total resting oxygen consumption of 3.5 × 70 = 245 mL/min, which is given in the problem as a baseline of 250 mL/min. The MET score of an activity is calculated by dividing the oxygen consumption during the activity by the baseline oxygen consumption.\nOxygen consumption during exercise = 1500 mL/min Baseline oxygen consumption = 250 mL/min MET score = (1500 mL/min) / (250 mL/min) = 6.\nTherefore, the activity has an energy expenditure that is six times the resting metabolic rate.",
