      "answerText": "(D) Clinical trial",
      "explanation": "Among all study designs, the Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT), a type of clinical trial, provides the strongest evidence for a cause-and-effect relationship. In an RCT, the investigator actively allocates an exposure (the \"cause,\" e.g., a new drug) to one group and a placebo or standard treatment to another, and then follows both groups to observe the outcome (the \"effect\"). The key features of randomization and a control group minimize confounding and bias, allowing for a strong inference of causality. Among observational studies, a cohort study (B) is the strongest design for assessing causality, as it establishes the temporal sequence (exposure precedes outcome). However, a well-conducted RCT is considered the gold standard and provides a higher level of evidence than any observational design.",
