      "explanation": "The child described has fever, difficulty swallowing, and swelling in the peritonsillar region, suggesting a peritonsillar abscess (quinsy). This condition involves infection and pus collection in the space between the tonsillar capsule and the superior constrictor muscle. Surgical drainage is commonly performed to relieve symptoms.\nPersistent bleeding after the procedure most commonly occurs due to injury to the paratonsillar vein, also known as the external palatine vein. This vein lies in close proximity to the lateral aspect of the tonsil, just outside the tonsillar capsule. Because of its superficial location in the peritonsillar space, it is particularly vulnerable during incision and drainage or tonsillectomy.\nThe tonsillar branch of the facial artery is deeper and less commonly injured. The ascending pharyngeal artery is smaller and located more posteriorly. The retromandibular vein is situated more laterally and is not directly related to the tonsillar bed.\nTherefore, in cases of persistent bleeding following drainage of a peritonsillar abscess, the most likely injured vessel is the paratonsillar vein.",
