      "explanation": "The image shows the anterior surface of the crystalline lens, visible through the pupil. The arrow is pointing to a circular ring of fine, brown pigment deposited on the anterior lens capsule. This specific finding is known as a Vossius ring. It is a pathognomonic sign of previous significant blunt trauma to the eye. The ring is formed when the force of the impact rapidly and forcefully compresses the iris against the anterior lens capsule. Pigment from the posterior epithelial layer of the iris is imprinted onto the lens in a circular pattern that matches the shape of the pupillary margin at the moment of impact. A Weiss ring (B) is a circular opacity seen in the vitreous humor, representing a posterior vitreous detachment. A Fleischer's ring (C) is an iron line at the base of the cone in keratoconus. A Kayser-Fleischer ring (D) is a copper deposit in Descemet's membrane seen in Wilson's disease.",
