      "explanation": "The fraction of inspired oxygen (FiO₂) delivered to a patient depends on the oxygen delivery device. A nasal cannula (D) is a low-flow device that provides an approximate FiO₂ of 24-44%. A Hudson mask (B), or simple face mask, is also a low-flow device delivering an FiO₂ of about 40-60%. A Venturi mask (A) is a high-flow device that can deliver a precise, fixed FiO₂ (e.g., 24%, 28%, 35%, 40%, 60%) by entraining a specific amount of room air. The device capable of delivering the highest possible FiO₂ via conventional therapy is the non-rebreathing mask (NRB). An NRB has a reservoir bag and one-way valves designed to minimize the mixing of exhaled air and room air with the oxygen supply. When run at a high flow rate (10-15 L/min), an NRB can deliver an FiO₂ of approximately 80% to over 95%, making it the device of choice for treating severe hypoxemia.",
