      "explanation": "This question relates to complications and follow-up after moderate to severe COVID-19, particularly in the context of the delta wave in India when mucormycosis was prevalent. Corticosteroid use, a mainstay of treatment, can cause hyperglycemia, so patients (especially diabetics) need to monitor their glucose levels (4) post-discharge. Steroids and the virus itself are immunosuppressive, increasing the risk of secondary infections. There was a major association between COVID-19 and invasive fungal sinusitis, particularly mucormycosis. Patients were advised to be vigilant for warning signs, which include headache, facial pain, nasal discharge (often blackish), and other sinusitis symptoms (3, 5). Persistent anosmia (2) is a common long-COVID symptom but not an urgent one to monitor for acute complications. Repeating the RT-PCR (1) is not recommended as it can remain positive for weeks due to non-viable viral fragments.",
