      "explanation": "The Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone Secretion (SIADH) is a disorder of impaired water excretion caused by the non-physiological release of ADH. This leads to the retention of free water, which dilutes the body's sodium, resulting in euvolemic hyponatremia. The diagnostic criteria include :\n(B) Hyponatremia with a corresponding low serum osmolality, so serum sodium < 135 mEq/L (B) is correct.\n(C) Inappropriately concentrated urine relative to the serum osmolality, so urine osmolality > 100 mOsm/kg (C) is correct.\n(D) The patient is typically clinically euvolemic (D), without signs of dehydration or significant edema.\nIn response to the water retention, the body tries to excrete sodium to maintain volume balance. This results in a high urinary sodium concentration, typically > 40 mEq/L. The statement that urinary sodium is < 20 mEq/L (A) is therefore incorrect. A low urinary sodium would be expected in hypovolemic hyponatremia.",
