      "explanation": "Cluster headache is an excruciatingly severe, unilateral headache with a rapid onset. The acute (abortive) treatment needs to be very fast-acting. Oral sumatriptan (A) has a slow onset of action and is generally ineffective for the rapid relief required. The two first-line abortive treatments are high-flow oxygen and injectable sumatriptan. Inhaling 100% oxygen via a non-rebreather mask is a highly effective treatment, but it must be administered at a high flow rate, typically 12 to 15 L/minute, not the lower rates of 6 or 8 L/minute mentioned in options C and D. The other first-line option, which is extremely effective and rapid, is subcutaneous sumatriptan (usually a 6 mg injection). Its rapid absorption provides relief within minutes. Given that the oxygen flow rates offered in the options are suboptimal, subcutaneous sumatriptan stands out as the most effective and correctly described first-line choice.",
