      "answerText": "(C) Anterograde amnesia with loss of recent memory",
      "explanation": "Korsakoff syndrome is a chronic neurological disorder that is often a sequela of an untreated episode of Wernicke's encephalopathy, both caused by severe thiamine (Vitamin B1) deficiency, typically in the setting of chronic alcoholism. The statement describing the triad of confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia (A) is the classic presentation of acute Wernicke's encephalopathy, not Korsakoff syndrome. The hallmark of Korsakoff syndrome is a profound and disproportionate memory deficit. The most prominent feature is severe anterograde amnesia, which is the inability to form new memories. Patients also have a significant retrograde amnesia affecting recent events more than distant ones. A characteristic feature is confabulation, where the patient invents memories to fill in the gaps. Immediate recall (digit span) and implicit memory (D) are often relatively preserved, as are other cognitive functions and personality (B).",
