      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Ergotism",
      "explanation": "The angiography image shows severe, diffuse, symmetrical narrowing and \"beading\" of the major arteries of the lower extremities. This appearance is characteristic of intense vasospasm. The clinical history provides the crucial link. The patient is HIV-positive and on antiretroviral therapy (ART). Certain classes of ART, particularly protease inhibitors (like ritonavir), are potent inhibitors of the cytochrome P450 3A4 enzyme. The patient also presents with a migraine, for which she may have taken an ergotamine-containing medication. Ergotamines are  also metabolized by CYP3A4. The concurrent use of a protease inhibitor and an ergotamine can lead to a dangerous drug-drug interaction. The protease inhibitor blocks the metabolism of the ergotamine, leading to toxic levels and causing severe, prolonged vasospasm, a condition known as ergotism. This vasospasm can lead to peripheral ischemia, causing the symptoms of numbness and potentially limb loss.",
