      "answerText": "(C) 3, 2, 4, 1",
      "explanation": "Acute iron poisoning classically follows a sequence of four or five distinct clinical stages :\n1. Stage 1 (0-6 hours) : This stage is dominated by the direct corrosive effects of iron on the gastrointestinal mucosa, leading to symptoms of gastrointestinal irritation (3), such as nausea, vomiting (often bloody), abdominal pain, and diarrhea.\n2. Stage 2 (6-24 hours) : This is the latent phase (2), a period of apparent clinical improvement where the initial GI symptoms may subside. This phase can be deceptive, as the patient is still absorbing iron and systemic toxicity is developing.\n3. Stage 3 (12-48 hours) : This stage is characterized by profound systemic toxicity, including shock, severe metabolic acidosis, coagulopathy, and end-organ damage, particularly liver failure and hypoglycemia (4).\n4. Stage 4 (2-6 weeks) : This is the late stage, where survivors may develop complications from healing with scarring (1), most notably gastric outlet obstruction or intestinal strictures.\nThe correct chronological sequence is 3 → 2 → 4 → 1.",
