      "explanation": "Hypokinesia refers to a poverty of movement, characterized by slowness (bradykinesia) and reduced amplitude and frequency of voluntary movements. Parkinson's disease (A) is the archetypal hypokinetic movement disorder. Catatonia (C) can present with profound hypokinesia, immobility, and posturing. Apraxia (B) is a disorder of motor planning, not movement initiation itself, but can result in a significant lack of spontaneous movement, appearing as hypokinesia. In contrast, chorea is a hyperkinetic movement disorder. It is characterized by involuntary, continuous, irregular, and non-repetitive flowing or \"dance-like\" movements that flit from one body part to another. It represents an excess of movement, the opposite of hypokinesia. Huntington's disease is a classic cause of chorea.",
