      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Liddle syndrome",
      "explanation": "This clinical picture describes a state of apparent mineralocorticoid excess. Liddle syndrome is a rare, autosomal dominant genetic disorder that perfectly fits this description. It is caused by a gain-of-function mutation in the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) in the collecting ducts of the kidneys. This mutation leads to excessive and unregulated reabsorption of sodium and, consequently, water. This volume expansion causes severe hypertension. The increased sodium reabsorption also creates a negative electrical potential in the tubular lumen, which drives the excessive secretion of potassium and hydrogen ions, resulting in hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. A key feature is that aldosterone and renin levels are suppressed. Bartter (A) and Gitelman (C) syndromes are salt-wasting tubulopathies that cause hypokalemia but with normal or low blood pressure. Gordon syndrome (D) causes hypertension but with hyperkalemia.",
