      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) C wave",
      "explanation": "The jugular venous pressure (JVP) waveform reflects pressure changes in the right atrium.\nThe 'a' wave (A) is caused by atrial contraction.\nThe 'c' wave is a small positive wave that occurs during early ventricular systole. It is thought to be caused by the bulging of the tricuspid valve back into the right atrium as it closes at the beginning of isovolumetric ventricular contraction. It can also have a component from the transmitted carotid artery pulsation.\nThe 'x' descent (C) is the fall in atrial pressure caused by atrial relaxation and the downward pulling of the tricuspid valve during ventricular systole.\nThe 'v' wave is caused by atrial filling against a closed tricuspid valve.\nThe 'y' descent (D) is the fall in atrial pressure when the tricuspid valve opens and the ventricle begins to fill.\nTherefore, the c wave corresponds to the isovolumetric contraction phase.",
