      "answerText": "(B) Paralysis",
      "explanation": "Compartment syndrome occurs when increased pressure within a closed fascial compartment compromises circulation and tissue perfusion. The classic clinical features are remembered as the “6 Ps” :\n1. Pain, Paresthesia, Pallor, Paralysis, Pulselessness, and Poikilothermia.\n2. The earliest and most sensitive sign is pain, especially pain out of proportion to injury and pain on passive stretch of the muscles within the affected compartment.\n3. As pressure increases, nerve ischemia develops, leading to paresthesia (early sensory change). With further compromise of arterial flow, pallor and diminished pulses may occur, although pulselessness is typically a late finding.\n4. Paralysis is a very late manifestation. It indicates prolonged nerve and muscle ischemia with possible irreversible damage. By the time paralysis appears, significant tissue necrosis may have already occurred, and urgent fasciotomy may not fully reverse deficits. Therefore, among the given options, the observation that manifests later in compartment syndrome is : (B) Paralysis.",
