      "answerText": "(B) Traction, palmar flexion, ulnar deviation, POP",
      "explanation": "A Colles' fracture is a fracture of the distal radius characterized by dorsal displacement, dorsal angulation, radial shift, and shortening. The closed reduction maneuver is designed to reverse these deformities in a specific sequence.\n1. Traction : The first step is to apply sustained longitudinal traction to the hand and wrist. This serves to disimpact the fracture fragments and restore the length of the radius.\n2. Palmar flexion : While maintaining traction, the distal fragment is pushed downwards into palmar flexion to correct the characteristic dorsal angulation of the fracture.\n3. Ulnar deviation : Next, the hand and wrist are pushed into ulnar deviation to correct the radial shift of the distal fragment.\n4. POP (Plaster of Paris) cast : Once the reduction is achieved and confirmed radiographically, a plaster cast is applied to hold the fracture in the corrected position while it heals.\nThis sequence ensures that each component of the deformity is addressed systematically before immobilization.",
