      "explanation": "The Thomas test is used to assess fixed flexion deformity of the hip.\nCorrect sequence:\n3. Look for exaggerated lumbar lordosis : The patient lies supine. First observe for increased lumbar lordosis, which suggests a possible hip flexion deformity.\n2. Flexion of the hip : Flex the normal (unaffected) hip to flatten the lumbar spine and eliminate lumbar lordosis.\n6. Overcorrect the normal side : Further flex the normal hip until the pelvis begins to rotate, ensuring complete correction of pelvic tilt.\n5. Ensure the ischial tuberosity makes contact with the ground : Confirm that the pelvis is stabilized and the lumbar spine is flat against the table (pelvis “squared”).\n4. Measure the actual flexion deformity : The angle between the affected thigh and the table is then measured—this represents the fixed flexion deformity.\nThus, the correct order is : 3 → 2 → 6 → 5 → 4, which corresponds to Option (C).",
