      "explanation": "The patient has difficulty flexing the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints of the 4th and 5th fingers and cannot hold a piece of paper between the fingers. This suggests ulnar nerve injury.\nUlnar nerve supplies:\n1. Flexor digitorum profundus (medial half) → flexion of DIP joints of 4th and 5th digits\n2. Interossei muscles → responsible for finger adduction (holding paper between fingers)\n3. Lumbricals (medial two)\nLoss of DIP flexion in the 4th and 5th digits indicates involvement proximal to the branch supplying flexor digitorum profundus (i.e., above the wrist).\nIn the image, point C corresponds to the medial epicondyle of the humerus, where the ulnar nerve passes posteriorly in the cubital tunnel. This is a common site of injury (“funny bone”).\nThus, injury at the medial epicondyle (label C) explains the symptoms.\nTherefore, the most likely site of injury is C (Option C).",
