      "answerText": "(A) Ulnar nerve",
      "explanation": "The image displays a classic claw hand deformity, specifically an ulnar claw hand, affecting the 4th and 5th digits. This deformity is caused by an injury to the ulnar nerve. The ulnar nerve innervates the medial two lumbricals (for the 4th and 5th fingers) and all of the interosseous muscles. The lumbricals are responsible for flexing the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints while extending the interphalangeal (IP) joints. When these intrinsic muscles are paralyzed, their actions are lost. The unopposed action of the long extensor muscles (innervated by the radial nerve) causes hyperextension at the MCP joints. Simultaneously, the unopposed action of the flexor digitorum profundus (partially innervated by the median nerve for these fingers in some cases, but the functional loss is key) causes flexion at the IP joints. This combination of MCP hyperextension and IP flexion creates the characteristic \"claw\" appearance of the affected fingers.",
