      "explanation": "The instruments shown in the image are used for performing a Pap smear (Papanicolaou test), which is a screening procedure for detecting premalignant and malignant lesions of the cervix. The set includes a vaginal speculum, Ayre’s spatula, endocervical brush, glass slides, and a fixative container.\nThe vaginal speculum is inserted into the vagina to visualize the cervix clearly. Once the cervix is exposed, the Ayre’s spatula is used to scrape cells from the ectocervix, particularly from the transformation zone where cervical dysplasia commonly develops. The endocervical brush is then inserted gently into the endocervical canal to collect columnar epithelial cells.\nThe collected cellular material is spread evenly onto glass slides and immediately placed in a fixative solution to preserve cellular morphology. These slides are later examined under a microscope to detect abnormal epithelial changes such as dysplasia, carcinoma in situ, or invasive carcinoma.\nThese instruments are not used for hysteroscopy, biopsy, or dilatation and curettage, as those procedures require different specialized tools. Therefore, the instruments shown are specifically used for a Pap smear.",
