      "explanation": "Uterocervical prolapse refers to the descent of the uterus and cervix through the vaginal canal due to weakness of the pelvic floor muscles and supporting ligaments such as the uterosacral and cardinal ligaments. It is commonly seen in multiparous, postmenopausal women and may present with a mass protruding per vaginum, pelvic pressure, backache, and urinary symptoms.\nIn a typical clinical image of uterocervical prolapse, a rounded mass is seen protruding from the vaginal introitus. The cervix may be visible at the most dependent part of the prolapsed mass, and in advanced stages, the entire uterus may descend outside the vaginal opening.\nCervical carcinoma usually presents as an irregular, ulcerative, friable growth on the cervix rather than a smooth descending mass. Uterine inversion typically occurs postpartum and presents as a sudden collapse with a fundal mass. A fibroid polyp appears as a localized pedunculated mass rather than complete uterine descent.\nThus, the most likely diagnosis is uterocervical prolapse.",
