      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Concealed sex.",
      "explanation": "Let's break down the clinical picture to arrive at the diagnosis :\n1. Patient Profile : An 18-year-old who appears female, with normal breast development and female external genitalia, but has never had a menstrual period (primary amenorrhea).\n2. Key Lab Test : She tests positive for 5-alpha-reductase deficiency.\n5-alpha-reductase is a crucial enzyme that converts testosterone into the much more potent dihydrotestosterone (DHT). DHT is essential for the development of male external genitalia during fetal development.\nIn an individual with a 46,XY (male) karyotype and 5-alpha-reductase deficiency :\n1. In Utero : The lack of DHT means the external genitalia do not masculinize, leading to the development of female-appearing external genitalia. These individuals are typically assigned female at birth and raised as girls.\n2. Internal Organs : They have testes (which may be undescended) that produce Anti-Müllerian Hormone, so they do not develop a uterus or fallopian tubes. This is why the patient has amenorrhea.\n3. At Puberty : The testes produce a surge of testosterone. This testosterone can be converted into estrogen, leading to the development of female breasts.\nThis entire scenario creates a discordance between the individual's genetic sex (male, XY) and their external physical appearance (female). This condition is what is being referred to by the somewhat outdated term \"Concealed sex\", as the male genetic identity is hidden by a female phenotype.\nWhy Other Options Are Incorrect\n(A) Mullerian agenesis : This affects 46,XX (female) individuals who are born without a uterus. They have normal ovaries and develop breasts, but have amenorrhea. This diagnosis is inconsistent with a 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, which affects 46,XY individuals.\n(C) AIS (Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome) : This is also a condition in 46,XY individuals who appear female. However, the cause is a defect in androgen receptors, meaning their body cannot respond to testosterone or DHT. While the physical presentation is very similar, the specific diagnosis of 5-alpha-reductase deficiency rules out AIS.\n(D) Turner's syndrome : This is caused by a 45,X0 karyotype. These individuals are female but have non-functional ovaries, leading to a lack of estrogen and failure to develop breasts without hormone therapy. This does not match the patient's presentation.",
