      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) Twin to twin transfusion syndrome",
      "explanation": "The ultrasound finding of polyhydramnios (excess amniotic fluid) in one twin and oligohydramnios (deficient amniotic fluid) in the other twin in a monochorionic pregnancy (twins sharing a single placenta) is the pathognomonic sign of Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome (TTTS). This serious complication arises due to unbalanced blood flow through inter-twin vascular anastomoses on the shared placental surface. One twin, the \"donor,\" transfuses blood to the other, the \"recipient.\" The donor twin becomes anemic and hypovolemic, leading to poor renal perfusion and oligohydramnios. The recipient twin becomes hypervolemic and polycythemic, leading to cardiac strain and polyuria, which results in polyhydramnios. This combination of oligohydramnios in the donor and polyhydramnios in the recipient is the defining feature of TTTS.",
