      "explanation": "The image shows a patch of complete depigmentation (loss of skin color) with well-defined borders. A key feature of the distribution of vitiligo is that it can be generalized or localized. When the depigmented patches are confined to one particular segment or dermatome on one side of the body, it is classified as segmental vitiligo. This type of vitiligo often has an early onset in childhood, spreads rapidly within that one segment, and then stabilizes. Focal vitiligo (A) refers to one or a few macules in a single location but without a dermatomal distribution. Piebaldism (C) is a congenital and stable genetic disorder characterized by a white forelock and symmetric white patches on the trunk and extremities, but not in a segmental pattern. A nevus depigmentosus (D) is also a congenital, stable patch of hypopigmentation, not complete depigmentation (amelanosis).",
