      "explanation": "The image shows a unilateral cluster of erythematous vesicular lesions distributed over one side of the chest in a dermatomal pattern. The lesions appear grouped and confined to a single dermatome, without crossing the midline. The patient also presents with pain, which is a key feature.\nHerpes zoster (shingles) occurs due to reactivation of latent varicella-zoster virus (VZV) in the dorsal root ganglia. When reactivated, the virus travels along the sensory nerve to the skin, producing painful vesicular eruptions along the affected dermatome. Thoracic dermatomes are most commonly involved.\n1. Molluscum contagiosum presents as painless, pearly, umbilicated papules.\n2. Herpes simplex usually causes grouped vesicles but commonly affects the oral or genital regions and does not typically follow a dermatomal pattern.\n3. Lymphangioma circumscriptum presents as clusters of translucent vesicles and is not typically painful.\nThe combination of unilateral dermatomal distribution and significant pain strongly supports herpes zoster as the diagnosis.",
