      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Tzanck cells",
      "explanation": "The image shows multiple grouped vesicular lesions on the genitalia, which are characteristic of genital herpes, most commonly caused by Herpes simplex virus type 2 (HSV-2), though HSV-1 can also be responsible. These lesions typically appear as clusters of fluid-filled vesicles on an erythematous base and may rupture to form painful ulcers.\nWhen scrapings are taken from the base of a vesicle and examined under microscopy using a Tzanck smear, the characteristic finding is multinucleated giant cells, known as Tzanck cells. These cells show ballooning degeneration and multinucleation due to viral cytopathic effects.\n1. Option (B), cells with a clear halo around the nucleus, refers to koilocytes seen in HPV infection.\n2. Option (C), molluscum bodies, are seen in molluscum contagiosum.\n3. Option (D), HP bodies, are associated with other viral inclusions, not HSV.\nThus, the most likely finding on scraping from these lesions is Tzanck cells, confirming herpes infection.",
