      "explanation": "Cardiac Cycle Sequence\nTo understand the correct order, let's trace the events that follow the P wave, which represents the electrical depolarization of the atria.\n1. P wave (Atrial Depolarization) → Atrial Contraction\ni. A. \"a\" wave : The contraction of the atria increases atrial pressure, causing a corresponding pressure wave in the jugular veins known as the \"a\" wave. This happens immediately after the P wave.\nii. C. Rapid filling of ventricles : The contracting atria push the final volume of blood into the ventricles. This phase is more accurately called \"late ventricular filling\" or the \"atrial kick,\" but it follows the \"a\" wave as the pressure from the contraction drives the blood flow.\n2. QRS Complex (Ventricular Depolarization) → Ventricular Contraction\nB. 1st heart sound (S1) : As the ventricles begin to contract (systole), the pressure inside them quickly rises above the pressure in the atria. This forces the atrioventricular (mitral and tricuspid) valves to snap shut, producing the first heart sound (\"lub\"). This event occurs after the QRS complex, which follows the P wave.\n3. T wave (Ventricular Repolarization)\nD. \"t\" wave : This wave on the ECG represents the repolarization (electrical reset) of the ventricles. It occurs during the latter part of ventricular contraction, after the first heart sound but before the second heart sound.\nTherefore, the correct chronological sequence of these events after the P wave is : \"a\" wave → filling of ventricles → 1st heart sound → \"t\" wave.",
