      "answerText": "(B) Denaturation → Annealing → Elongation",
      "explanation": "The Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is a technique used to amplify a specific segment of DNA exponentially. It consists of a series of repeated cycles, with each cycle comprising three distinct temperature-controlled steps :\n1. Denaturation : The reaction mixture is heated to a high temperature (typically 94-98°C). This breaks the hydrogen bonds holding the double-stranded DNA template together, separating it into two single strands.\n2. Annealing : The temperature is lowered (typically 50-65°C). This allows short, single-stranded DNA primers, which are complementary to the sequences flanking the target region, to bind (anneal) to their respective sites on the single-stranded DNA templates.\n3. Elongation (or Extension) : The temperature is raised again (typically to 72°C), which is the optimal temperature for the thermostable DNA polymerase (like Taq polymerase). The polymerase then synthesizes a new complementary DNA strand, starting from the primers and extending along the template strand.\nThis three-step cycle is repeated 25-35 times, leading to an exponential amplification of the target DNA sequence.",
