      "statement": "Compare the normality of 2 mM HCl and 1 mM H₂SO₄.",
      "options": [
        "Same",
        "Double",
        "Half",
        "Four times"
      ],
      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Same",
      "explanation": "Normality (N) is a measure of concentration defined as the molarity (M) of a solution multiplied by its equivalence factor (or n-factor). For an acid, the n-factor is the number of moles of hydrogen ions (H⁺) that one mole of the acid can donate.\nFor hydrochloric acid (HCl), it is a monoprotic acid, meaning it donates one H⁺ ion per molecule. Therefore, its n-factor is 1.\n1. Normality of 2 mM HCl = 2 mM × 1 = 2 mN (millinormal).\n2. For sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄), it is a diprotic acid, meaning it can donate two H⁺ ions per molecule. Therefore, its n-factor is 2.\n3. Normality of 1 mM H₂SO₄ = 1 mM × 2 = 2 mN.\nSince both solutions have a normality of 2 mN, their normalities are the same.",
