      "explanation": "This is a duplicate of a previous question. The thermic effect of food (TEF), also called specific dynamic action, is the energy expenditure required for the digestion, absorption, and metabolism of nutrients after a meal. This value varies significantly between the different macronutrients. Proteins have the highest TEF, with about 20-30% of their ingested energy being used for their processing and assimilation. This is due to the energy-intensive processes of deamination and urea synthesis. Carbohydrates (B) have an intermediate TEF, typically in the range of 5-10%. Lipids (A), or fats, have the lowest TEF, at only about 0-3%, meaning they are the most metabolically efficient macronutrient to store. Therefore, protein consumption leads to the greatest increase in metabolic rate compared to the other macronutrients, a principle often utilized in diets for weight management.",
