      "explanation": "The chromosomal translocation t(11;14) is the genetic signature of Mantle Cell Lymphoma (MCL). This genetic event is pivotal because it leads directly to the over expression of a key protein.\nB. Cyclin D1 : The t(11;14) translocation places the CCND1 gene (which codes for Cyclin D1) next to a powerful gene enhancer. This results in the massive overexpression of Cyclin D1, a protein that pushes the cell cycle forward. It's the defining molecular feature of MCL.\nD. CD200 : This is a cell surface glycoprotein. While not as fundamental as Cyclin D1, research shows that CD200 is expressed in a notable subset of MCL cases (around 25-40%).\nIn contrast, CD10 (C) is a marker typically found in other B-cell lymphomas like Follicular Lymphoma and is characteristically negative in MCL. While SOX11 (A) is also a key diagnostic marker for MCL, option (D) is the best choice as it correctly pairs the essential marker (Cyclin D1) with another valid finding (CD200).",
