      "answerText": "(B) Hairy cell leukemia.",
      "explanation": "All four options are valid causes of pancytopenia.\nMegaloblastic anemia, Acute promyelocytic leukemia, and Myelodysplastic syndrome all disrupt the bone marrow's ability to produce sufficient healthy blood cells, leading to low counts.\nHowever, Hairy cell leukemia is uniquely characterized by this presentation. The disease involves malignant B-lymphocytes (the \"hairy cells\") infiltrating the bone marrow.  This infiltration, combined with the significant marrow fibrosis (scarring) it causes, physically crowds out and suppresses the normal production of red cells, white cells, and platelets.\nTherefore, pancytopenia is a hallmark and nearly universal finding at the time of diagnosis for Hairy cell leukemia, often accompanied by a massively enlarged spleen. This makes it a quintessential example of a disease presenting with pancytopenia.",
