      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) 5-HT agonist",
      "explanation": "The primary classes of drugs used for the acute treatment of migraine are the triptans (e.g., sumatriptan) and the ergot alkaloids (e.g., ergotamine). The mechanism of action of both classes involves agonism at serotonin (5-HT) receptors, specifically the 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D subtypes. Their therapeutic effect is mediated by constricting dilated intracranial blood vessels and inhibiting the release of pro-inflammatory neuropeptides in the trigeminal system. However, these drugs are not entirely selective, and their agonist activity at 5-HT1B receptors located on coronary arteries can cause vasoconstriction of the coronary arteries. In susceptible individuals, particularly those with underlying coronary artery disease, this vasoconstriction can be significant enough to induce myocardial ischemia, leading to angina-like symptoms such as chest pain, tightness, or pressure. This is a well-known and important potential side effect of these drugs.",
