      "explanation": "The currently available Human papillomavirus vaccine is based on the L1 major capsid protein of the human papillomavirus (HPV). The L1 protein has the ability to self-assemble into virus-like particles (VLPs). These VLPs resemble the outer shell of the virus but do not contain viral DNA, making them non-infectious and safe.\nWhen administered, these L1-based VLPs stimulate a strong neutralizing antibody response. The antibodies prevent HPV from attaching to and infecting epithelial cells, thereby providing protection against cervical cancer and other HPV-related cancers.\nThe early proteins E6 and E7 are oncogenic proteins responsible for malignant transformation (they inactivate tumor suppressor proteins p53 and Rb). However, they are not used in current prophylactic vaccines because they are expressed after infection and are associated with oncogenic activity.\nTherefore, HPV vaccines such as Gardasil and Cervarix are based on the L1 capsid protein, not E6 or E7.",
