      "explanation": "This is a 13-year-old minor and therefore a child under the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act (POCSO Act). Sexual assault of a minor is a mandatory reportable offence, and healthcare providers are legally obligated to inform the police. Hence, informing the police (2) is compulsory, even if police have not accompanied the victim.\nSince the child is refusing medical examination, consent is essential before any examination or sample collection. Under medico-legal and ethical guidelines, no physical examination or evidence collection (such as vaginal sampling) can be done without valid consent. Therefore, recording refusal of consent (3) in detail is mandatory.\nHowever, even if examination is refused, the doctor has a duty to provide necessary emergency medical care, including prophylaxis for sexually transmitted diseases and pregnancy prevention, as these are in the best interest of the child. Thus, starting treatment for STD and pregnancy (1) is appropriate.\nTaking vaginal samples (4) and starting examination (5) cannot be done without consent.\nTherefore, the correct combination is 1, 2, and 3 (Option A).",
