      "explanation": "The image shows a characteristic branching, greenish-black discoloration pattern over the skin, resembling a marble-like network. This is known as marbling, a postmortem change seen during decomposition.\nMarbling occurs due to the breakdown of hemoglobin in blood vessels by bacteria during putrefaction. Hydrogen sulfide produced by bacterial action reacts with hemoglobin to form sulfhemoglobin, which stains the superficial veins. This produces a distinct reticular (branching) pattern along the course of subcutaneous veins.\nIt is an early sign of putrefaction and commonly appears over the abdomen and chest.\nThe other options are incorrect:\n1. Tattooing refers to punctate abrasions caused by unburnt gunpowder.\n2. Mummification is drying and preservation of the body in arid conditions.\n3. Embalming is an artificial preservation process.\nSince the image clearly shows venous pattern discoloration typical of decomposition, the process is marbling.",
