      "explanation": "Audiological tests can be classified as objective or subjective (behavioral). Objective tests do not require a behavioral response from the patient; they measure a physiological response. Brainstem Evoked Response Audiometry (BERA) or ABR (A), Impedance Audiometry (including tympanometry and acoustic reflex testing) (C), and otoacoustic emissions are all objective tests. Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT) (B) is an objective imaging test used in ophthalmology, not audiology. In contrast, Pure Tone Audiometry (PTA) is a subjective or behavioral test. It requires the patient to listen to tones of specific frequencies and intensities and provide a conscious response (e.g., by raising a hand or pressing a button) when they hear the tone. The results depend entirely on the patient's perception and cooperation, making it a subjective measure of hearing thresholds.",
