      "number": 117,
      "printedNumber": 117,
      "paperSession": "Nov 2020",
      "statement": "What is the first and specific marker for myocardial infarction (MI)?",
      "options": [
        "LDH",
        "Troponin",
        "CK (Creatine Kinase)",
        "None of the above"
      ],
      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) Troponin",
      "explanation": "Cardiac troponins (specifically troponin T and troponin I) are regulatory proteins that are components of the contractile apparatus of cardiac muscle. They are highly specific to the myocardium. When myocardial cells are damaged and undergo necrosis during a myocardial infarction (MI), these proteins are released into the bloodstream. The measurement of serum cardiac troponin levels is the current gold standard and the most sensitive and specific biochemical marker for the diagnosis of MI. Troponins begin to rise in the blood within 2-4 hours of the onset of injury, peak at 12-24 hours, and can remain elevated for 7-14 days. While Creatine Kinase (CK), especially the CK-MB isoenzyme, was previously used, it is less specific than troponin as it is also found in skeletal muscle. LDH (A) is even less specific and is no longer used for diagnosing MI.",
      "pages": [
        699,
        739
      ],
      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "difficultyEstimated": true,
      "topic": "General Medicine"
