      "answerText": "(B) a, b, c, d.",
      "explanation": "All four instruments shown are used in the management of bleeding esophageal varices. Here is a breakdown of each :\na & b. Balloon Tamponade Tubes : These are Sengstaken-Blakemore tubes (or similar variants like the Minnesota tube). They are inserted into the esophagus and stomach. The device has two balloons : a gastric balloon that is inflated and pulled taut to anchor it, and a long esophageal balloon that is then inflated to apply direct pressure (tamponade) on the bleeding varices. This is a temporary, life-saving measure used to control severe, uncontrolled hemorrhage.\nc & d. Flexible Endoscopes : These are standard upper gastrointestinal (GI) endoscopes. Endoscopy is the primary and definitive method for both diagnosing and treating esophageal variceal bleeding. Through the endoscope, a gastroenterologist can perform procedures like :\nEndoscopic Variceal Ligation (EVL or Banding) : The preferred treatment, where small rubber bands are placed around the varices to cut off their blood supply.\nSclerotherapy : Injecting a solution into the varices to make them clot and shrink.\nSince both balloon tamponade devices (a, b) and endoscopes (c, d) are crucial tools for stopping esophageal variceal bleeding, the correct option includes all four.",
