      "explanation": "This 35-year-old man has acute abdominal pain and obstruction, and the erect abdominal X-ray shows free air under the diaphragm, indicating pneumoperitoneum. In a patient with a history of peptic ulcer disease, this strongly suggests perforated peptic ulcer, which is a surgical emergency.\nManagement steps:\n(a) USG abdomen – Imaging helps confirm free fluid and assess intra-abdominal pathology. However, once free air is seen on X-ray, diagnosis is usually clear.\n(b) IV fluids – Immediate resuscitation is essential. These patients are at risk of dehydration, sepsis, and shock. IV fluids, nasogastric decompression, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and proton pump inhibitors should be started.\n(c) Exploratory laparotomy – This is the definitive management. A perforated peptic ulcer requires urgent surgical repair to prevent generalized peritonitis and septic shock.\nObservation alone is inappropriate because perforation is life-threatening and requires urgent intervention.\nTherefore, the correct next steps include investigation, resuscitation, and emergency surgery — option (A).",
