      "explanation": "The CEAP (Clinical, Etiological, Anatomical, Pathophysiological) classification is the standard system for describing chronic venous disorders. The \"C\" component describes the clinical signs on a scale from 0 to 6.\n1. C0 : No visible or palpable signs of venous disease.\n2. C1 : Telangiectasias or reticular veins.\n3. C2 : Varicose veins.\n4. C3 : Edema.\n5. C4 : Changes in the skin and subcutaneous tissue secondary to chronic venous disease. This class is further subdivided :\ni. C4a : Pigmentation or eczema.\nii. C4b : Lipodermatosclerosis or atrophie blanche.\niii. C4c : Corona phlebectatica.\n6. C5 : Healed venous ulcer.\n7. C6 : Active venous ulcer. Since the patient has both eczema and lipodermatosclerosis, he falls under the C4 clinical category.",
