      "correct": "D",
      "answerText": "(D) Myopectineal orifice is bounded by Iliopubic tract inferiorly",
      "explanation": "In laparoscopic inguinal anatomy, understanding the Myopectineal Orifice of Fruchaud is vital for successful hernia repair. The statement in (D) is false because the inferior boundary of this orifice is the pectineal ligament (Cooper’s ligament) and the superior pubic ramus, not the iliopubic tract.\nThe iliopubic tract actually serves as a key landmark for the Triangle of Pain, forming its lateral and superior border. This triangle is clinically significant because it contains the lateral femoral cutaneous and genitofemoral nerves; placing surgical tacks here can lead to chronic nerve entrapment.\nConversely, the Triangle of Doom is defined medially by the vas deferens and laterally by the spermatic vessels. Surgeons must avoid this zone to prevent life-threatening injury to the external iliac artery and vein. Finally, the superior boundary of the Myopectineal Orifice consists of the arching fibers of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles. Mastering these boundaries allows surgeons to safely navigate the \"Circle of Death\" and ensure mesh coverage over all potential hernia sites.",
