      "number": 137,
      "printedNumber": 137,
      "paperSession": "Nov 2020",
      "statement": "What is the Tillaux sign in mesenteric cysts?",
      "options": [
        "Mass is mobile only in plane perpendicular to mesentery",
        "Mass is brilliantly transilluminating",
        "Mass felt in the peri-umbilical region",
        "Fluctuation positive"
      ],
      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Mass is mobile only in plane perpendicular to mesentery",
      "explanation": "A mesenteric cyst is a rare, benign, fluid-filled sac located within the mesentery of the small intestine. A key clinical feature that helps in its diagnosis is its characteristic mobility on physical examination. This is known as Tillaux's sign (or Tillaux's triad, which also includes a dull percussion note and a location in the umbilical region). The sign describes that the mass is mobile in the direction perpendicular to the line of attachment of the mesentery, but its mobility is restricted in the direction parallel to the mesenteric root. The root of the small bowel mesentery runs obliquely from the duodenojejunal flexure in the left upper quadrant down to the ileocecal junction in the right lower quadrant. Therefore, the cyst can be moved from side-to-side (perpendicular to this line) but not up-and-down (parallel to this line). This specific pattern of mobility is highly suggestive of a mesenteric origin.",
