      "paperSession": "Nov 2020",
      "statement": "If a doctor is inserting screws into the vertebrae, where will these screws be placed?",
      "options": [
        "Pedicle",
        "Lamina",
        "Spine",
        "Facet joints"
      ],
      "correct": "A",
      "answerText": "(A) Pedicle",
      "explanation": "In modern spinal fusion and instrumentation surgery, the most common and biomechanically robust method for achieving fixation to the vertebrae is through the use of pedicle screws. The pedicle is a strong, cylindrical pillar of bone that connects the posterior elements (lamina, facets) of a vertebra to the vertebral body. Placing a screw down the central axis of the pedicle and into the vertebral body allows for a rigid, three-column purchase on the vertebra. This provides a very strong anchor point to which rods can be attached to stabilize the spine, correct deformities, or facilitate fusion. While other fixation methods exist (e.g., laminar hooks or screws, facet screws), pedicle screw instrumentation is the workhorse of contemporary spinal surgery due to its superior strength and versatility. \"Spine\" (C) is too general.",
