      "explanation": "The sagittal CT scan of the spine shows extensive calcification and ossification of the spinal ligaments, particularly the anterior longitudinal ligament, and fusion of the facet joints, leading to the bridging of adjacent vertebral bodies. This gives the spine a rigid, fused appearance that is classically described as a \"bamboo spine.\" This radiographic finding is the pathognomonic sign of advanced ankylosing spondylitis (AS). AS is a chronic, inflammatory autoimmune disease that primarily affects the axial skeleton, including the sacroiliac joints and the spine. The progressive inflammation leads to fibrosis and ossification, causing the characteristic stiffness, loss of mobility, and eventual fusion of the spine. The patient's symptoms of increasing stiffness and difficulty with forward bending are also classic for this condition. The other conditions listed do not produce this specific bamboo spine appearance.",
