      "correct": "C",
      "answerText": "(C) 1,3,4",
      "explanation": "Osteoporosis is characterized by reduced bone mineral density (BMD) and increased fracture risk. The investigations used aim to measure bone density and evaluate metabolic causes.\n1. DEXA (Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry) is the gold standard for diagnosing osteoporosis. It measures bone mineral density at the hip and spine and provides a T-score for assessment.\n2. Quantitative CT (QCT) is another method that measures volumetric bone density and can assess trabecular bone separately. Though less commonly used due to higher radiation exposure and cost, it is a valid diagnostic tool.\n3. Chemical analysis refers to biochemical investigations such as serum calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, vitamin D, and parathyroid hormone levels. These tests help identify secondary causes of osteoporosis.\n4. Bone scan (nuclear medicine scan) is not used to diagnose osteoporosis. It detects areas of increased bone turnover, such as metastasis, infection, or fractures, but does not measure bone density.\nThus, the correct combination is 1, 3, and 4 (Option C).",
