      "number": 148,
      "printedNumber": 148,
      "paperSession": "Nov 2020",
      "statement": "If all parts of the sternum and the medial end of the clavicle are ossified, what is the estimated age?",
      "options": [
        "18-25 years",
        ">25 years",
        "14-15 years",
        "< 15 years"
      ],
      "correct": "B",
      "answerText": "(B) >25 years",
      "explanation": "This question uses key skeletal maturation milestones for age estimation. The sternum ossifies from several centers. The fusion of the manubrium with the body (manubriosternal joint) occurs late in life, but the fusion of the four sternebrae of the body is typically complete by 25 years of age. The xiphoid process also fuses with the body later. The most critical landmark provided is the medial clavicular epiphysis. This is one of the last epiphyses in the entire body to fuse. The fusion process begins around age 18-22 and is generally complete by 25 to 31 years of age. Therefore, if both the sternum and the medial clavicle are observed to be fully ossified and fused, it is a strong indicator that the individual is older than 25 years. This milestone is particularly useful in forensic anthropology for determining if an individual has reached adulthood.",
