      "explanation": "Different dilutions of adrenaline (epinephrine) are used for specific clinical applications and routes of administration to ensure safety and efficacy.\n1. 1 : 1,000 (1 mg/mL) is the standard concentration for intramuscular (IM) or subcutaneous injection, most commonly used for treating anaphylaxis. (1-B)\n2. 1 : 10,000 (0.1 mg/mL) is the more dilute preparation used for intravenous (IV) or intracardiac (IC) administration, primarily during cardiac arrest resuscitation (ACLS protocol). (2-A)\n3. 1 : 100,000 or 1 : 200,000 (0.01 mg/mL) is a very dilute concentration that is combined with local anesthetics (e.g., lidocaine). The adrenaline acts as a vasoconstrictor, which prolongs the duration of the anesthetic block and reduces systemic absorption and bleeding. (3-C)\nThe correct matching sequence is therefore 1-B, 2-A, and 3-C.",
