      "explanation": "Certain clinical features at the time of diagnosis of schizophrenia are associated with a better or worse long-term prognosis. Good prognostic factors include a late age of onset (A), an acute or abrupt onset of symptoms, the presence of obvious precipitating factors, good premorbid functioning, a family history of mood disorders, and a clinical picture dominated by positive symptoms (D) (like hallucinations and delusions) rather than negative symptoms. The presence of significant mood symptoms, such as depression (B), during the psychotic episode is also associated with a better outcome. In contrast, an insidious onset (C), where symptoms develop gradually and subtly over a long period, is a well-established poor prognostic factor. It is often associated with a clinical picture dominated by negative symptoms, poor premorbid adjustment, and a more chronic, deteriorating course.",
